00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:35.780
GUEST SPEAKER: --to Abe, who
is going to give us a guest

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.930
lecture on art games.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:41.760
And today's reading, the proctor
reading is kind of

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.620
like part two of a lot of the
things that Jason maybe talked

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.170
about when we did simulations.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.450
If you guys remember, is a
quality reading that a

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.074
[INAUDIBLE] question, where
that came from.

00:00:54.074 --> 00:00:57.800
One thing that I think was of
interest was that there are a

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.430
couple of responses
to that article.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:03.510
If you scroll all the way down
to the bottom of the article

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:04.879
that these responses
vary [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.010
back to them.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:11.320
And those are kind of
interesting, because it just

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:12.410
carries on a conversation.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:16.665
People actually just turning his
article into basically a

00:01:16.665 --> 00:01:18.600
blog post and then
write on it.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:19.640
And they were pretty good.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.340
But a lot of that was kind
of the state of art game

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.635
discussion back in 1999, I
think, I think a pretty old

00:01:27.635 --> 00:01:32.660
article, where they were very
much thinking about games have

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:33.540
to be simulations.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.920
Games and narratives, blaaach.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:41.330
Storytelling is like some
other mediums' domain.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:42.580
It's all about the simulation.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:49.890
Arguably at that time there
wasn't a big discussion of an

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:50.490
art games movement.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:54.040
There were other games
movements, like new games

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:58.160
which were really more about
people interacting with you,

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.940
trying to get games to where
people were more cooperative,

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:04.300
less competitive, and
less cutthroat.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:09.840
There were other movements
about people--

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.200
they were probably advertising
game movements.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:17.270
And there was a lot of work
in people making months of

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:19.940
computer games that effectively
made it

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:23.210
unplayable, but made really
pretty images in the process.

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:25.590
So that [UNINTELLIGIBLE], for
instance, where everything

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.620
becomes black and white
and is striped,

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:28.460
including the monsters.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:30.770
There's no way you can see what
the hell's going on, but

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:33.494
they look pretty cool.

00:02:33.494 --> 00:02:34.530
AUDIENCE: Have you seen--

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.904
I think it's 96 kilobytes--

00:02:37.904 --> 00:02:41.290
there was a competition in full
game [INAUDIBLE]-- no

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:42.150
it's 4 kilobytes, I think.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:45.328
It's a 4-kilobyte game in full
3-D, with multiple levels,

00:02:45.328 --> 00:02:47.830
extremely clever, including
the music and everything.

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:50.210
PROFESSOR: Yeah, yeah.

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:51.500
AUDIENCE: Have you ever heard
of this or seen this?

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.070
GUEST SPEAKER: There are
a couple of those.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.010
A lot of those come up
with the demoscene.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:58.770
Demoscene, which I will put
off to some other time--

00:02:58.770 --> 00:02:59.310
AUDIENCE: It's very
impressive, too.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:00.120
It's like very--

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.940
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, yeah.

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:05.100
It's kind of this weird thing
of computer hacking, really,

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.740
really fast and really, really
compact coding, and visualized

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:11.990
statics and music, which
goes more like a

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:13.890
digital music video.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:17.360
Only there were some people who
added mouse control, and

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.210
game play into it and made
these kind of games.

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:24.790
But that's very different from
the art game scene that we

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:26.980
have today, or at least
what's commonly

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:27.810
described as art games.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:34.140
So I'd like Abe to talk about
what happened since then,

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:34.940
since 1999.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:36.461
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, yeah.

00:03:36.461 --> 00:03:37.383
Hi, guys.

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:38.305
I'm Abe.

00:03:38.305 --> 00:03:39.770
I work here at the lab.

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:40.800
I play a bunch of your games.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.210
I've met a bunch of you.

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:44.020
It's nice to be here.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:46.550
Phillip asked me to come
in to talk about,

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:48.150
quote/unquote, art games.

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:54.720
I think largely because it's a
kind of, I'll say, sector of

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.210
game development that my work
at Gambit tends to intersect

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:02.100
with, especially recently.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:05.563
And I think, too, that it's--

00:04:05.563 --> 00:04:06.024
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

00:04:06.024 --> 00:04:09.440
I was choking from--

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:13.410
GUEST SPEAKER: I think, too,
that it's a sector that's a

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:15.860
little bit confusing--

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:17.220
and possibly confusing, too.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:20.680
And so, just to give
you guys a sense--

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.745
when you asked me about this
it actually made me think,

00:04:23.745 --> 00:04:25.705
like, oh, yeah, cool, I'll
talk about this art games

00:04:25.705 --> 00:04:28.410
movement or whatever,
but that of course--

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:29.270
ooh, wait.

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:33.070
I'm locked into some kind
of weird mode right now.

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:34.020
Sorry.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:35.222
OK.

00:04:35.222 --> 00:04:38.040
That brings up this question,
which is, are games art?

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:44.200
And just to give you a sense
for how this little talk

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.660
thing, whatever, is going to go,
I'm going to talk a little

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:50.020
bit about this question, give
you examples of what people

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:53.661
have said about this, and
opinions that I think are

00:04:53.661 --> 00:04:54.420
particularly important,
particularly

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.880
valid on this subject.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:01.040
I also after that want to show
you some examples, some

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:03.200
historical and then kind of
moving forward examples.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.910
I'm actually going to get some
of you guys to just come up

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.020
here and maybe even play some of
these examples that I think

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.020
are particularly important.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:13.322
And I'd really like it if we
can keep this less of a me

00:05:13.322 --> 00:05:17.150
talking at you and you engaging
me as you have

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:17.840
questions on this.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:23.420
Because me blabbing at you as
you fall asleep is maybe good

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:28.050
for a nap, but less useful for
getting anything out of this.

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:30.750
So we'll try to structure this
as a conversation, and then

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:34.500
I'll try to pull back, hearken
back, to any of my teacher

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.640
instincts and see if I
can get you engaged.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:37.670
But we'll see.

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.060
So are games art?

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:41.878
I hate this question.

00:05:41.878 --> 00:05:45.240
Let's start off by saying that
I fundamentally find it

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.490
abhorrent, and I hear
it all the time.

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:51.560
And it's aggravating to me
because I think it's

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:53.330
incontrovertibly yes.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:56.730
I think that you can't possibly
come up with any

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.800
other answer other than yes,
games are art, games have art,

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:01.090
yes, yes, yes, right?

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:03.410
But it keeps coming up.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:06.380
And it keeps becoming a
question-- and I should say,

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:11.910
too, that this is a wholly
biased and slanted talk.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:16.110
This is my perspective on this,
and hopefully by the

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:17.640
time-- you'll be like, wow,
he's absolutely right.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:20.870
There's no way that games could
be anything but art.

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:23.790
Or at least they are
art, and then some.

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:27.580
So this guy, who's a little
hard to see right now, but

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:30.300
he's handily brought up,
recently brought into the

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:35.920
fore, and that's Roger Ebert
of Siskel and Ebert fame.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:38.205
Or since you guys are, some of
you, younger than me, probably

00:06:38.205 --> 00:06:40.850
Ebert and Roeper.

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:42.430
He had some interesting,

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:44.960
controversial comments recently.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:47.200
And I pulled this quote,
"Nevertheless, I'm being

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:49.040
convinced that in principle--"
I love that

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:51.070
he put that in italics.

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:52.770
"--video games cannot be art.

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:55.630
Perhaps it is foolish for me to
say never, because never as

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:58.380
Rick Wakeman informs us,
is a long, long time.

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:02.020
Let me just say that no video
gamer now living will survive

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:05.400
long enough to experience the
medium as an art form."

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:08.760
So apparently we should all be
dead already, according to

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:10.650
Ebert, because we wouldn't
have lived long enough to

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:12.020
experience the medium
as an art form.

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:15.610
But here's a guy who, even
though I don't think he's a

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:17.580
particularly good film critic,
is a popular film critic.

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:19.910
And he's weighing in on a
medium that he doesn't

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:20.340
particularly understand.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:23.240
And I think it's important to
look at what exactly is he

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:25.980
doing when he makes
this statement.

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:28.510
He's in this business of
classification, right?

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:30.130
He's in this business of saying,
all right, we've got

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:31.480
this subject called art.

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:35.570
What can we say falls under that
subset and what does not?

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:37.450
So he says things like painting,
sculpture, dance,

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:42.580
opera, literature, film can all
be classified as art and

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:46.110
that games are not.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:47.430
Probably some other things.

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:48.930
I don't know where he stands
on comic books.

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:50.765
I don't think he's written about
it yet, but I'm sure

00:07:50.765 --> 00:07:51.190
he'd probably--

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:54.100
I don't know what he thinks
about pornography.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:56.190
Although, since it's in
film form, maybe he

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:56.520
does think it's art.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:57.230
It's unclear.

00:07:57.230 --> 00:07:59.650
He hasn't weighed
in on that yet.

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:01.120
But the interesting
thing about this

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:02.700
is, this is not new.

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:04.850
This is like a high-culture,
low-culture debate that's been

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:06.550
going on for a really
long time.

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:08.560
And I think it's particularly
hilarious because

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:09.840
he would use films.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:12.560
And films had to fight out of
the low-culture phenomenon to

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:15.020
gain respect as a high-culture
medium.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:17.160
So he's working--

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:19.860
reviewing a medium that was once
conceived as low culture,

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:22.580
had to work really hard to be
considered high culture.

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:25.620
And now he's saying, look, my
films are high culture, your

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:26.770
video games are low culture.

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:30.350
Which is totally hilarious
because in the past 50 years,

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:32.780
this has been completely
collapsed by post-modernism.

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:36.031
That's a whole 'nother
talk altogether.

00:08:36.031 --> 00:08:36.640
Maybe high culture
and low culture

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:38.360
don't even exist anymore.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:39.950
So Ebert brings us all back.

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:40.780
And you get everybody upset.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:42.840
And a bunch of trolls are on
his website saying really

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:45.240
terrible things about him
and about what he said.

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:48.450
But he kind of reinvigorated
this talk.

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:51.180
And so new scientists, inspired
by this Ebert

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:54.630
controversy and looking for con
tips on CultureLab asked

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:56.570
the question, can
games be art?

00:08:56.570 --> 00:08:58.880
And they sent it out to a bunch
of game scholars saying,

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:01.725
what is your opinion on this?

00:09:01.725 --> 00:09:06.340
Ian Bogost is a very smart
guy and says some really

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:06.850
interesting things.

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:08.420
This is really why I'm not gonna
read the whole thing,

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:13.170
but in the first paragraph he
brings up things like Fountain

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:16.550
by Marcel Duchamp, which is a
urinal in a museum, for those

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:17.400
of you who don't
know about it.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:20.340
And that was a statement, a
piece of art that he was

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:23.020
literally trying to confront,
what high culture defines as

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:28.520
art by taking, literally, a
urinal, putting it upside down

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:30.270
on a pedestal, entitling it
Fountain, and putting it in a

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:32.180
museum, which is the pinnacle
of high culture.

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:34.530
If you can make it in a
museum, you're art.

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:36.996
And so this is something
that--

00:09:36.996 --> 00:09:40.220
He mentions Yoko Ono's
performance art, where she's

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:43.247
sitting in a chair, and she has
scissors next to her and

00:09:43.247 --> 00:09:45.510
the audience is invited to come
cut her clothes off one

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:47.500
piece at a time.

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:49.270
A lot of this kind of stuff.

00:09:49.270 --> 00:09:52.480
Andy Warhol pop culture
paintings,

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:55.950
Mondrian's abstract paintings.

00:09:55.950 --> 00:09:58.260
And he says a lot in that
first paragraph.

00:09:58.260 --> 00:09:59.860
But I think this interesting
part that I've

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:01.730
pseudo-highlighted down
here is interesting.

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:02.940
I'm starting in the middle.

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:05.670
He says if the purpose of art
is indeed to force us to see

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:08.560
something we thought we
understood in a new light,

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:10.995
perhaps the most fundamental
move video games have made in

00:10:10.995 --> 00:10:14.151
the artistic tradition is in
the very eliciting of the

00:10:14.151 --> 00:10:15.640
question, can video
games be art?

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:17.000
I think that's really
interesting.

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:19.030
So the fact that people are
even asking this question

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:22.380
suggests that video games are
doing something that we

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:25.070
traditionally conceive
of art doing.

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:27.470
That's, I think, a really
powerful statement, that we're

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:31.840
even having this discussion
renders the question moot in

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:35.080
some senses, which
is I would say

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:39.698
classic Bogostian wordplay.

00:10:39.698 --> 00:10:41.626
I think I just made something
up called Bogostian.

00:10:41.626 --> 00:10:44.730
I should tweet all about it.

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:48.070
John Sharp, from the Savannah
College of Art and Design, has

00:10:48.070 --> 00:10:50.050
had a lot of thoughts on this.

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:51.950
He's actually a great person to
talk about this, because he

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:53.870
studies video games, but he does
it from an art history

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:54.940
background as well.

00:10:54.940 --> 00:10:56.750
That's pretty good and useful.

00:10:56.750 --> 00:11:00.310
He talks about some interesting
things here.

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:03.750
Similarly to what Ian is doing,
he's talking a bit

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:08.340
about how video games and art
are doing some similar things

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:11.510
at times, and yet games have
certain affordances that other

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:14.638
media do not.

00:11:14.638 --> 00:11:17.440
He says at the end here, which
I think is interesting, all

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:19.990
this poses the question of why
are we concerned with giving

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:21.220
video games the status of art?

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:23.010
This is one of the reasons
why I hate this question.

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:24.940
Are we simply trying
to legitimize them?

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:27.000
Does being called, quote/unquote
art change the

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:28.730
qualities of video games.

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:31.056
Only if we can answer these
questions can we move the

00:11:31.056 --> 00:11:31.620
conversation forward.

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:34.500
I think he's reacting here
to the question itself.

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:36.730
Actually, he and Ian both are
reacting to the question, Ian

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:39.150
saying the question invalidates
itself and John

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:42.820
Sharp now saying that this
question, it's not helping us.

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:45.850
Asking how does calling it art,
what does that do for the

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:48.390
medium, that's the important
question.

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:51.470
Here's some thoughts about the
question and some more

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:54.340
evidence for why I think the
question, are video games art

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:57.898
is just quite wrong.

00:11:57.898 --> 00:12:03.880
Mary Flanagan takes it as being
assumed that video games

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:05.170
are art, which I think
is actually great.

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:08.150
In Critical Play she doesn't go
to great lengths to define

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:08.910
video games as art.

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:10.710
She just looks at art and
she looks at games

00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:11.830
and compares them.

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:14.580
She just assumes that
this is so.

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:17.500
I think this is a really nice
quote from the beginning of

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:19.520
her Critical Play book, which
is that, "Critical Play is

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:22.180
built on the premise that, as
with other media, games carry

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:24.620
beliefs within their
representation systems and

00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:26.440
mechanics." This is really
important. "Artists using

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:29.012
games as a medium of
expression--" Again, good use

00:12:29.012 --> 00:12:30.290
of italics.

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:32.230
"--,then, manipulate elements
common to games--

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:34.836
representation systems and
styles, rules of progress,

00:12:34.836 --> 00:12:37.640
codes of conduct, context of
reception, winning and losing

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:40.442
paradigms, ways of interacting
in a game-- for they are the

00:12:40.442 --> 00:12:43.220
material properties of games,
much like marble and chisel or

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:46.025
pen and ink bring with them
their own intended

00:12:46.025 --> 00:12:48.350
possibilities, limitations,
and conventions."

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:49.280
It's a great point.

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:51.860
You would have a really hard
time making a painting with a

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:55.177
chisel and no paint, like you
were using marble and a

00:12:55.177 --> 00:12:56.410
chisel, a you were trying
to make a painting.

00:12:56.410 --> 00:12:57.360
Two different things.

00:12:57.360 --> 00:12:58.130
Both are art.

00:12:58.130 --> 00:12:59.670
It's the same thing
with games.

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:03.490
Games have rule sets, games
have systems, games have

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:04.730
win-and-loss paradigms.

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:07.955
They have all these things
that are conventions and

00:13:07.955 --> 00:13:11.180
things that we associate with
even our definition of what a

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:14.280
game is, which is
at times faulty.

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:15.890
But those are our tools.

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:17.060
Those are how we communicate
things.

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:20.150
I think her first sentence
is really important.

00:13:20.150 --> 00:13:22.210
I'm guessing with the stuff
that you've been talking

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:24.372
about-- them and Gonzalo
Frasca--

00:13:24.372 --> 00:13:25.670
that's coming through.

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:27.190
I actually see that
in your games.

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:29.450
You're trying to-- so, for
example I play-tested that

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:32.800
FedEx game, and I play-tested
the game about marriage.

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:35.290
There's all kinds of meaning in
those systems and meanings

00:13:35.290 --> 00:13:39.200
in those choices that you get
that are being communicated.

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:42.220
And that's very similar to what
an artist would do with

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:43.156
any other medium.

00:13:43.156 --> 00:13:45.310
You're communicating a message
through those.

00:13:45.310 --> 00:13:47.120
And sometimes we do
it accidentally.

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:49.200
It's not always explicit.

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:49.940
We make something--

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:54.020
I would say that the graveyard
and when you get divorced and

00:13:54.020 --> 00:13:56.610
you put your divorced person
in the graveyard is an

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:59.360
interesting choice for me,
because it was creating an

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:01.473
equivalence between divorce and
death, which I think is

00:14:01.473 --> 00:14:02.980
kind of interesting.

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:05.540
That's just one interesting
example.

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:08.790
So Mary Flanagan in her book
talks about this art movement

00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:09.210
called Fluxus.

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:09.980
Before I go into--

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:13.550
any kind of thoughts
or questions?

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:15.680
Does anybody else think-- does
anybody think video games

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:16.833
aren't art still?

00:14:16.833 --> 00:14:17.520
It's okay.

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:19.544
Oh, crap, we're gonna fight.

00:14:19.544 --> 00:14:22.125
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] when we
were designing art, so our art

00:14:22.125 --> 00:14:25.590
project now is you have
to choose a real-world

00:14:25.590 --> 00:14:27.072
system and relate it.

00:14:27.072 --> 00:14:28.740
That's what we're
all working on.

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:31.570
And one of the big things that
really helped us get started

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:34.303
is, what is the message we want
to get across to each set

00:14:34.303 --> 00:14:34.580
of ablutions?

00:14:34.580 --> 00:14:35.370
What is a bad ablution?

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:36.826
Do you want to say that's
random, do you want to say

00:14:36.826 --> 00:14:37.690
that's this?

00:14:37.690 --> 00:14:40.910
And how do we express that,
which [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:41.225
GUEST SPEAKER: Totally.

00:14:41.225 --> 00:14:43.640
And it gets even crazier
when you take--

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:45.760
when you try to look at
systems that are not

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:48.590
particularly tangible.

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:52.500
You maybe looked at it, but
Elude is a game about

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:53.780
depression.

00:14:53.780 --> 00:14:57.135
There aren't exactly tangible
kind of road maps the way

00:14:57.135 --> 00:15:00.540
there are with FedEx systems,
for clinical depression.

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:02.570
And yet you're looking at
things like emotions,

00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:06.660
feelings, stuff like that, that
we might call abstract,

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:08.410
or just not immediate.

00:15:08.410 --> 00:15:12.205
How do you communicate
those in systems?

00:15:12.205 --> 00:15:13.630
It's really tricky.

00:15:13.630 --> 00:15:16.955
AUDIENCE: I have friends that
talk about all their games

00:15:16.955 --> 00:15:21.626
which are like more --genic
or more abstract.

00:15:21.626 --> 00:15:23.900
Does Ebert [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
art?

00:15:23.900 --> 00:15:27.075
GUEST SPEAKER: I mean, it's
a really good question.

00:15:27.075 --> 00:15:28.860
AUDIENCE: What would
he say about that?

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:31.400
GUEST SPEAKER: I think that
falls along the same lines as,

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:34.460
like I said, does Ebert consider
pornography art?

00:15:34.460 --> 00:15:35.790
It's a really difficult
question.

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:41.460
That's a much larger art history
debate, about has high

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:43.200
culture and low culture
completely collapsed?

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:47.870
Has mass-media proliferation
deteriorated conceptions of

00:15:47.870 --> 00:15:51.110
what really high culture art
are, versus low culture.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:56.700
I don't have particularly
good answers for that.

00:15:56.700 --> 00:16:00.000
I think just as all of the other
media that we looked at,

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.200
does a Jerry Bruckheimer
film count as art?

00:16:02.200 --> 00:16:05.100
Just like we would look at that
and ask that question, we

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:08.720
don't then deny the entire
medium of film from the

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:10.600
possibility of being artistic.

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:13.310
There seems to be this desire,
because the majority of games

00:16:13.310 --> 00:16:14.900
in our sector--

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:16.780
which I actually do,
too, because if you

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:18.290
go far enough back--

00:16:18.290 --> 00:16:21.000
I should say video games--

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:24.847
Because most video games are
commercially intended and

00:16:24.847 --> 00:16:28.280
involve fairly similar things,
does that preclude the medium

00:16:28.280 --> 00:16:30.904
from the possibility
of being artistic?

00:16:30.904 --> 00:16:35.140
I go back to that Ebert comment
about he says they

00:16:35.140 --> 00:16:38.160
can't possibly be, or at least
he says never is a long time.

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:41.556
But not in our lifetime, it
apparently is impossible.

00:16:41.556 --> 00:16:42.720
It's a good point.

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:46.600
AUDIENCE: There's actually
interesting effects with the

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:49.263
game we're designing which is
essentially city building and

00:16:49.263 --> 00:16:53.380
city creation in the time
of the Roman Empire.

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:57.845
And we actually didn't
intend to instill any

00:16:57.845 --> 00:16:58.880
meaning in the game.

00:16:58.880 --> 00:17:01.090
But once we actually designed
the mechanics and stuff, we

00:17:01.090 --> 00:17:02.940
realized that it was essentially
a game about the

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:05.760
rich getting richer and trying
to control the board.

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:08.170
So it's like there's almost
this inherent meaning that

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:09.780
came out from just designing
the game.

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:10.859
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, yeah.

00:17:10.859 --> 00:17:14.050
You almost can't help but have
it happen in some instances.

00:17:14.050 --> 00:17:17.449
It reminds me of a good thing
that Brenda Brathwaite put in

00:17:17.449 --> 00:17:19.589
one of her talks once, which
was about the game

00:17:19.589 --> 00:17:22.560
Civilization, the mod
Colonization for sig IV, which

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:24.970
is a great mod in which you
play one of the European

00:17:24.970 --> 00:17:26.910
colonizers and you come
over to the New World.

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:30.530
And there's indigenous
peoples there.

00:17:30.530 --> 00:17:32.450
You have to negotiate with them
at first, but eventually

00:17:32.450 --> 00:17:36.010
you just beat them up and take
their land, or they just give

00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:37.450
it to you because you're more
powerful than they are.

00:17:37.450 --> 00:17:40.160
And she was making the point,
there's this great box art,

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:42.490
the Native Americans in the
corner and the Conquistador in

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:44.290
the middle, the Spanish,
and the Dutch.

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:46.380
And it's like, if you were a
Native American how would you

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:47.610
respond to this game?

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:50.850
And I'm like, yeah, I'm totally
making America again.

00:17:50.850 --> 00:17:53.160
And other people would be like,
oh my god, you basically

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:56.080
just wiped out my ancestors,
thanks.

00:17:56.080 --> 00:17:59.610
So it's true, sometimes you
falling into meaning.

00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:00.918
It's something.

00:18:00.918 --> 00:18:03.720
And I think, again, a lot
of art does that, right?

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:06.620
And some art did it quite
purposely, where they weren't

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:10.860
divulging the meaning, they were
inviting readings on it.

00:18:10.860 --> 00:18:11.310
So, Fluxus.

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:14.770
Fluxus is this really
interesting

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:16.690
mid-20th-century art--

00:18:16.690 --> 00:18:19.160
they rejected the term
"movement," they called it an

00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:20.264
"attitude"--

00:18:20.264 --> 00:18:23.200
that Mary Flanagan talks
about a bit in her

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:24.280
Critical Play book.

00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:25.820
It's actually quite
interesting.

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:28.410
It really starts out early
with John Cage.

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:30.940
Have you guys heard of
him, the composer?

00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:34.400
He's most famous for this piece
of music that he wrote

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:37.240
where, I forget the duration,

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:38.130
AUDIENCE: 4'33".

00:18:38.130 --> 00:18:39.020
GUEST SPEAKER: 4'33", yeah.

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:44.070
it's 4 minutes and 33 seconds
of not, well, not playing.

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:44.740
AUDIENCE: He rests.

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:45.030
GUEST SPEAKER: He rests.

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:45.930
Exactly.

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:48.560
So you'd sit down
at the piano.

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:50.050
You'd pull out the music and
everyone would hush.

00:18:50.050 --> 00:18:52.340
And then for 4 minutes and 33
seconds you'd sit there,

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:54.310
possibly with your hands
on the keys.

00:18:54.310 --> 00:18:56.566
And then after the time, you
would stand up, and you'd take

00:18:56.566 --> 00:18:58.670
your bow, and the audience
would erupt

00:18:58.670 --> 00:19:02.370
into incredible applause.

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:06.110
He was really testing the idea
of what they eventually called

00:19:06.110 --> 00:19:07.680
scores, or event scores.

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:13.090
This idea that music is an art
form where you can write it

00:19:13.090 --> 00:19:15.060
down, and then it becomes
performable.

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:19.130
It becomes this thing that
someone can engage with.

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:21.280
We don't think of paintings
that way.

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:23.750
We don't think of paintings as
having a score, having some

00:19:23.750 --> 00:19:26.060
kind of, how do I engage
with this?

00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:29.790
It sounds kind of like games,
in figuring out how does

00:19:29.790 --> 00:19:33.790
interactivity work in
artistic mediums.

00:19:33.790 --> 00:19:37.080
Really interested in
indeterminacy, too.

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:40.230
I think you'll see in some of
these examples, they're really

00:19:40.230 --> 00:19:44.480
kind of pushing on the
boundaries of what art has

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:46.010
been doing to this point.

00:19:46.010 --> 00:19:50.910
They call it intermedia, meaning
that the more multiple

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:55.060
modes that you can use for
communicating it was really

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:57.520
important to the attitude.

00:19:57.520 --> 00:20:01.390
Simplicity was really
big, having basic

00:20:01.390 --> 00:20:03.450
systems and basic scores.

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:07.590
And then this idea of play
was really important.

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:11.020
Yoko Ono, it's funny we sort
of just attach her to John

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:15.405
Lennon, but she was an artist
before she was in a

00:20:15.405 --> 00:20:16.270
relationship with him.

00:20:16.270 --> 00:20:18.920
She's actually well known as
one of these kind of Fluxus

00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:20.650
practitioners.

00:20:20.650 --> 00:20:21.440
This is interesting.

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:24.460
This is the Fluxus manifesto.

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:27.090
I wanted to put this up because
I think you can get a

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:30.940
little bit of a sense for what
they were trying to go for.

00:20:30.940 --> 00:20:34.810
They were taking the word
"flux" and putting

00:20:34.810 --> 00:20:37.370
definition-- but then they
were mending it here, so,

00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:41.310
purge the world of Bourgeois
sickness, intellectual,

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:43.220
professional, and commercialized
culture.

00:20:43.220 --> 00:20:46.320
Purge the world of dead art,
imitation, artificial art,

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:48.150
abstract art, illusionistic
art, mathematical art.

00:20:48.150 --> 00:20:51.762
I like this, purge the
world of Europeanism.

00:20:51.762 --> 00:20:53.578
I don't think that's a word.

00:20:53.578 --> 00:20:55.370
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:20:55.370 --> 00:20:56.120
GUEST SPEAKER: Promote
a revolutionary

00:20:56.120 --> 00:20:58.000
flood and tide in art.

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:01.280
Promote living art, anti-art,
non-art reality.

00:21:01.280 --> 00:21:03.860
These things sound
totally crazy.

00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:06.720
I love the, "to be fully
grasped," but then it crosses

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:10.380
out "fully." I don't doubt
that was completely

00:21:10.380 --> 00:21:12.440
intentional, or if they wrote
fully grasped and were like,

00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:13.345
oh, this is gonna be great.

00:21:13.345 --> 00:21:14.709
I'm crossing it out so
you can see that I

00:21:14.709 --> 00:21:15.180
crossed out fully grasped.

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:17.790
We want it to be not
fully grasped.

00:21:17.790 --> 00:21:20.860
We want it to be partially
grasped by all people.

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:24.690
Not only critics feel this
class is professional.

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:27.370
It's just a really interesting
thing, and it sort of screams

00:21:27.370 --> 00:21:28.520
the avant-garde.

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:30.080
They were trying to push
against boundaries.

00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:32.420
They're trying to figure out
what is the edge of the medium

00:21:32.420 --> 00:21:35.770
and then how can we
jump way off it?

00:21:38.450 --> 00:21:41.190
Some of the classic Fluxus works
were these things called

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:43.630
Flux boxes or Flux kits.

00:21:43.630 --> 00:21:45.270
Flux kits tended to
have event scores.

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:46.580
This is one of the most
famous called Water

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:48.870
Yam by George Brecht.

00:21:48.870 --> 00:21:53.930
And basically this is a box
with 70 event scores.

00:21:53.930 --> 00:21:56.460
These are an example of
these event scores.

00:21:56.460 --> 00:21:56.940
It's interesting.

00:21:56.940 --> 00:21:59.160
The event scores really seem
like rule systems.

00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:02.070
They're like the instructions
to your board game, except

00:22:02.070 --> 00:22:03.803
they're kind of whacky.

00:22:03.803 --> 00:22:06.960
If you look at air conditioning,
the rule is move

00:22:06.960 --> 00:22:08.150
through the place.

00:22:08.150 --> 00:22:09.880
Which is really interesting.

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:12.522
If you encounter this game and
you're like, all right, I'm

00:22:12.522 --> 00:22:13.612
gonna play Water Yam.

00:22:13.612 --> 00:22:15.540
And you're like, move
through the place.

00:22:15.540 --> 00:22:18.840
I think that's pretty
characteristic of movement,

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:22.540
right, present you with some
sort of objective or some sort

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:27.601
of requirement, but one that
is abstract or nebulous, or

00:22:27.601 --> 00:22:28.870
just kind of strange.

00:22:28.870 --> 00:22:30.400
This one's even better.

00:22:30.400 --> 00:22:34.490
On a white chair, a great tape
measure, alphabet, flag,

00:22:34.490 --> 00:22:36.920
black, and spectral color.

00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:38.680
In this instance, it's not
even clear what you're

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:39.920
necessarily supposed to do.

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:43.390
Are you supposed to compile
these things on a white chair?

00:22:43.390 --> 00:22:45.370
Are you supposed to sit on a
white chair and hold all of

00:22:45.370 --> 00:22:46.521
these things?

00:22:46.521 --> 00:22:48.485
It's not particularly clear.

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:52.810
This is the playfulness
of this movement.

00:22:52.810 --> 00:22:59.870
This is the energy of this
of avant-garde movement.

00:22:59.870 --> 00:23:02.350
You can see the relationship,
I hope, between

00:23:02.350 --> 00:23:03.800
rule systems and these.

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:06.386
The next example is, I think,
an even better one.

00:23:06.386 --> 00:23:07.910
But you can see how
they're playing

00:23:07.910 --> 00:23:08.560
around with these ideas.

00:23:08.560 --> 00:23:10.625
At least they're incorporating
interactivity

00:23:10.625 --> 00:23:12.070
into these art movements.

00:23:12.070 --> 00:23:14.987
And keep in mind, these are
being shown in galleries These

00:23:14.987 --> 00:23:17.740
are being shown in
high-art places.

00:23:17.740 --> 00:23:23.285
These are being sent out to
their friends as works of art.

00:23:23.285 --> 00:23:24.640
This one's great.

00:23:24.640 --> 00:23:28.560
This is White Chess
by Yoko Ono.

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:31.490
It's often depicted on its own
and you have to imagine what

00:23:31.490 --> 00:23:31.990
the rules are.

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:34.510
But I actually think her rules
as they are written are

00:23:34.510 --> 00:23:36.010
actually pretty important
to it.

00:23:36.010 --> 00:23:38.780
She says, play it for as long
as you remember who is your

00:23:38.780 --> 00:23:41.640
opponent and who is
your own self.

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:44.750
Very interesting wordage.

00:23:44.750 --> 00:23:46.665
What are the complications
with white chess.

00:23:46.665 --> 00:23:47.160
Anyone.

00:23:47.160 --> 00:23:47.440
Any thoughts?

00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:50.750
Based on your perceptions
of non-white chess or

00:23:50.750 --> 00:23:52.070
white-and-black chess?

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:55.070
AUDIENCE: You'd get into arguing
about is that my pawn

00:23:55.070 --> 00:23:56.210
or your pawn?

00:23:56.210 --> 00:23:57.760
GUEST SPEAKER: Yes, you
definitely-- it will be hard

00:23:57.760 --> 00:23:59.560
to keep track of,
I would think.

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:01.670
That's why she said, "as long
as you can remember." Any

00:24:01.670 --> 00:24:04.920
other weirdness from this?

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:06.150
AUDIENCE: Who is friend
and who is enemy?

00:24:06.150 --> 00:24:07.620
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah,
certainly.

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:09.400
This sort of like, wait,
was that my piece?

00:24:09.400 --> 00:24:10.100
Was that your piece?

00:24:10.100 --> 00:24:13.200
Not only does that complicate
your relationship to the other

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:15.670
player, but now it's like, to
the game itself, it's like,

00:24:15.670 --> 00:24:17.864
wait, what was the strategy
I was doing?

00:24:17.864 --> 00:24:18.640
AUDIENCE: Friendly or
unfriendly fire?

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:20.472
GUEST SPEAKER: I mean, it's
really hard to keep track of

00:24:20.472 --> 00:24:21.150
stuff in chess.

00:24:21.150 --> 00:24:22.480
What about the board?

00:24:22.480 --> 00:24:24.170
AUDIENCE: He writes off the king
and the queen by mistake

00:24:24.170 --> 00:24:25.730
because you don't have to--

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:28.880
AUDIENCE: There's not really any
actual boxes on the board.

00:24:28.880 --> 00:24:30.780
AUDIENCE: There are boxes--

00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:33.740
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah,
they're all white.

00:24:33.740 --> 00:24:36.915
That complicates things a little
bit, too, because you

00:24:36.915 --> 00:24:38.870
need to remember how
you were moving.

00:24:38.870 --> 00:24:39.220
AUDIENCE: How large is--

00:24:39.220 --> 00:24:39.712
GUEST SPEAKER: This
one's really big.

00:24:39.712 --> 00:24:42.410
This is like a garden
installment of White Chess.

00:24:42.410 --> 00:24:45.600
And I think the cool thing, too,
this is another example

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:47.020
of the movement.

00:24:47.020 --> 00:24:48.690
As long as you have
the components you

00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:49.710
could play this game.

00:24:49.710 --> 00:24:51.090
You could create
this art piece.

00:24:51.090 --> 00:24:54.190
I think the intent
is not go play my

00:24:54.190 --> 00:24:55.560
installation of White Chess.

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:58.410
It's that this is the
rules, these are the

00:24:58.410 --> 00:25:00.400
pieces, it can be played.

00:25:00.400 --> 00:25:01.850
That screams game,
that screams that

00:25:01.850 --> 00:25:04.540
portability of a game.

00:25:04.540 --> 00:25:08.380
Again, this wasn't considered
anything but art, except

00:25:08.380 --> 00:25:10.750
probably by people who
turned their nose at

00:25:10.750 --> 00:25:14.260
this sort of thing.

00:25:14.260 --> 00:25:20.100
they've Any thoughts on
what White Chess could

00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:23.740
be about, in a--

00:25:23.740 --> 00:25:26.150
AUDIENCE: Two different
sides merging to one.

00:25:26.150 --> 00:25:31.500
And so you eventually get,
why am I fighting?

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:33.170
Who is my enemy in
this situation?

00:25:33.170 --> 00:25:33.620
GUEST SPEAKER: Sure, sure.

00:25:33.620 --> 00:25:35.440
Any other thoughts?

00:25:35.440 --> 00:25:36.560
AUDIENCE: Racial relations.

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:38.830
GUEST SPEAKER: Sure,
racial relations.

00:25:38.830 --> 00:25:41.730
AUDIENCE: It's probably a whole
antiwar thing of, like,

00:25:41.730 --> 00:25:43.560
are we actually destroying
ourselves?

00:25:43.560 --> 00:25:45.920
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, and that's
an interesting thing.

00:25:45.920 --> 00:25:50.290
Yoko Ono was a decidedly antiwar
activist at the time

00:25:50.290 --> 00:25:52.790
she was making this
in the early '60s.

00:25:52.790 --> 00:25:54.975
One thing-- you can't take
anything for granted, but the

00:25:54.975 --> 00:25:57.060
pieces aren't all black,
they're all white.

00:25:57.060 --> 00:26:00.179
So I think the race thing
does play a factor.

00:26:00.179 --> 00:26:02.892
The inability to remember
who you're attacking and

00:26:02.892 --> 00:26:04.220
who you are, even.

00:26:04.220 --> 00:26:06.320
I think that's actually why
I really like her words.

00:26:06.320 --> 00:26:08.801
Because, "for as long as you
can remember who's your

00:26:08.801 --> 00:26:10.170
opponent and who is your own
self," there's something about

00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:13.650
incorporating your own self in
there, not just saying what

00:26:13.650 --> 00:26:14.710
your pieces are.

00:26:14.710 --> 00:26:16.740
She's not saying can you
remember what your pieces are.

00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:19.100
She's saying remember who you're
playing against versus

00:26:19.100 --> 00:26:20.996
who you are.

00:26:20.996 --> 00:26:25.370
That's very different
verbiage.

00:26:25.370 --> 00:26:28.680
I think it's very much
a game about who are

00:26:28.680 --> 00:26:30.250
you fighting, anyway?

00:26:30.250 --> 00:26:34.428
What is the point of war, what
is the purpose of war?

00:26:34.428 --> 00:26:37.230
And how does war particularly
function?

00:26:37.230 --> 00:26:39.116
And in the rhetoric of
the time it makes a

00:26:39.116 --> 00:26:41.670
lot of sense, right?

00:26:41.670 --> 00:26:44.140
So that's Yoko Ono's White
Chess, which is part of the

00:26:44.140 --> 00:26:45.260
Fluxus [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:26:45.260 --> 00:26:46.620
Yes, it's pretty cool.

00:26:46.620 --> 00:26:49.566
Actually, Fluxus and artists in
this period have a really

00:26:49.566 --> 00:26:51.180
interesting relationship
with chess.

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:52.526
There's like some quote,
and I'm gonna

00:26:52.526 --> 00:26:53.640
butcher it, but it's--

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:57.690
I think it was Marcel Duchamp
saying that not all chess

00:26:57.690 --> 00:27:00.350
players are artists, but all
artists are chess players, or

00:27:00.350 --> 00:27:00.980
something like that.

00:27:00.980 --> 00:27:04.050
He is particularly fascinated
with chess, and you can see

00:27:04.050 --> 00:27:08.020
elements of chess in
his work, ought to

00:27:08.020 --> 00:27:10.860
be painted or whatever.

00:27:10.860 --> 00:27:12.110
AUDIENCE: He was completely
obsessed with chess.

00:27:12.110 --> 00:27:12.830
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah.

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:14.805
AUDIENCE: He played
it really hard.

00:27:14.805 --> 00:27:17.140
He wasn't necessarily
a very good player,

00:27:17.140 --> 00:27:18.120
but he played a lot.

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:20.080
GUEST SPEAKER: Very into it.

00:27:20.080 --> 00:27:22.830
So now I'm gonna move out
of this Fluxus movement.

00:27:22.830 --> 00:27:24.075
I just wanted to show you
a couple examples.

00:27:24.075 --> 00:27:27.091
But I think this next one is
actually an interesting-- this

00:27:27.091 --> 00:27:29.300
is a current theme that I'm
really excited about, which is

00:27:29.300 --> 00:27:32.436
called Sixteen Tons.

00:27:32.436 --> 00:27:34.700
This is a game I unfortunately
have not been able to play

00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:37.070
yet, but I've witnessed
play because it's a

00:27:37.070 --> 00:27:37.900
game for four players.

00:27:37.900 --> 00:27:42.640
This was created by Eric
Zimmerman and Nathalie Pozzi.

00:27:42.640 --> 00:27:43.560
It's a really interesting
game.

00:27:43.560 --> 00:27:45.230
It's a little hard to see
from these pictures.

00:27:45.230 --> 00:27:47.490
But it's this giant circle, I
would say almost the size of

00:27:47.490 --> 00:27:50.775
this room, of corrugated
cardboard, maybe even bigger,

00:27:50.775 --> 00:27:52.350
a little bit bigger
than this room.

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:57.840
Inside you've got this array
of colored and circles in

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:04.010
these pretty hefty steel
tubes that have color.

00:28:04.010 --> 00:28:04.920
Let's go through the rules.

00:28:04.920 --> 00:28:07.330
The first thing you notice is
that they contextualize the

00:28:07.330 --> 00:28:11.790
title, Sixteen Tons, with a
quote from the song, "You load

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:13.412
16 tons, and what do you get?

00:28:13.412 --> 00:28:15.070
Another day older and
deeper in debt."

00:28:15.070 --> 00:28:18.766
This is doing an interesting
thing that John Sharp, who you

00:28:18.766 --> 00:28:20.510
can see in the middle, thinks
is actually particularly

00:28:20.510 --> 00:28:23.710
important for games and games
that are working this art

00:28:23.710 --> 00:28:26.370
movement is that they declare
their intentionality.

00:28:26.370 --> 00:28:29.570
Whether they declare what
they're meaning is or not is

00:28:29.570 --> 00:28:30.640
less important.

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:33.650
To be taken seriously, you take
yourself seriously, I

00:28:33.650 --> 00:28:35.030
think was one of
things he said.

00:28:35.030 --> 00:28:38.450
And I think this is another
good example when you talk

00:28:38.450 --> 00:28:40.600
about how games maybe in the
digital space do this.

00:28:40.600 --> 00:28:44.480
But right now they're already
priming you for reading the

00:28:44.480 --> 00:28:47.245
rules in a way that is different
than just, ooh,

00:28:47.245 --> 00:28:48.750
let's play.

00:28:48.750 --> 00:28:52.260
Could you imagine if Monopoly
had some sort of Marxist quote

00:28:52.260 --> 00:28:53.940
in the front of it, or something
like that, your

00:28:53.940 --> 00:28:57.360
experience of it would be
completely different.

00:28:57.360 --> 00:29:00.460
You set up, you put the pieces
in this array, you stand on a

00:29:00.460 --> 00:29:02.750
number and that indicates
what color you are.

00:29:02.750 --> 00:29:04.480
And this is great, you
take out $3.00.

00:29:04.480 --> 00:29:08.180
Although you can see in this
second picture they're playing

00:29:08.180 --> 00:29:12.485
what Eric the designer has
deemed high stakes.

00:29:12.485 --> 00:29:16.625
Sixteen Tons where you pull out
three five-dollar bills.

00:29:16.625 --> 00:29:17.930
It says, how to win.

00:29:17.930 --> 00:29:19.740
You win when the two pieces
of your color are directly

00:29:19.740 --> 00:29:21.460
adjacent to each other.

00:29:21.460 --> 00:29:24.142
So diagonally adjacent does
not count for a win.

00:29:24.142 --> 00:29:25.660
The way you play is, players
take turns, starting with

00:29:25.660 --> 00:29:26.285
player one.

00:29:26.285 --> 00:29:28.320
When it's your turn, you
say, put me to work.

00:29:28.320 --> 00:29:30.530
Players may then offer to
pay you one or more

00:29:30.530 --> 00:29:31.880
dollars in any order.

00:29:31.880 --> 00:29:34.060
So anyone can just say,
I'll give you this.

00:29:34.060 --> 00:29:36.130
You have to accept the highest
payment, except

00:29:36.130 --> 00:29:37.730
if there's a tie.

00:29:37.730 --> 00:29:40.420
Because with specific
denominations there is very

00:29:40.420 --> 00:29:41.140
likely to be a tie.

00:29:41.140 --> 00:29:43.570
Then you get to choose
which of the highest

00:29:43.570 --> 00:29:46.110
bidders you can take.

00:29:46.110 --> 00:29:50.700
The person who pays then tells
you what move to make.

00:29:50.700 --> 00:29:52.480
And if no one offers to
pay you, you get to

00:29:52.480 --> 00:29:53.991
decide what to do.

00:29:53.991 --> 00:29:56.877
And then it's the next player's
turn, and you would

00:29:56.877 --> 00:29:59.980
continue until one
player wins.

00:29:59.980 --> 00:30:02.000
Just off the top of your heads,
having not even played

00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:04.370
it-- it's tough to critique
games when you haven't played.

00:30:04.370 --> 00:30:06.302
But just looking at the
rules and hearing

00:30:06.302 --> 00:30:06.850
about it and even--

00:30:06.850 --> 00:30:09.970
I love that picture on the left,
because it embodies some

00:30:09.970 --> 00:30:12.550
of the spirit of
the play, too.

00:30:12.550 --> 00:30:14.755
What are some interesting things
about this rule set of

00:30:14.755 --> 00:30:17.330
that you notice?

00:30:17.330 --> 00:30:19.630
AUDIENCE: You play with money,
but you won't win money?

00:30:19.630 --> 00:30:21.650
GUEST SPEAKER: Well, that's
an interesting question.

00:30:21.650 --> 00:30:25.434
What happens to the money
at the end of the game?

00:30:25.434 --> 00:30:25.915
AUDIENCE: Who knows?

00:30:25.915 --> 00:30:26.396
Do you keep it?

00:30:26.396 --> 00:30:27.839
AUDIENCE: It's unclear.

00:30:27.839 --> 00:30:29.550
GUEST SPEAKER: Very unclear.

00:30:29.550 --> 00:30:31.620
That's one of the things that's
really great about this

00:30:31.620 --> 00:30:35.470
game, is that it puts you to
into these social situations

00:30:35.470 --> 00:30:37.190
where now you're playing
with real currency

00:30:37.190 --> 00:30:38.210
that was your own.

00:30:38.210 --> 00:30:42.160
And now it's like, what
happens to this?

00:30:42.160 --> 00:30:44.600
It says you win if your piece--
but maybe you feel

00:30:44.600 --> 00:30:46.790
like you win if you moved those
pieces and you have the

00:30:46.790 --> 00:30:48.500
most money at the
end of the game.

00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:51.620
You've got this kind of
weird economy in here.

00:30:51.620 --> 00:30:54.697
I think the fact that
it's not explicit in

00:30:54.697 --> 00:30:56.230
this rule set is great.

00:30:56.230 --> 00:30:59.590
That's what creates this
potential for an interesting

00:30:59.590 --> 00:31:00.380
social dynamic.

00:31:00.380 --> 00:31:02.870
Other things that are
interesting about this game?

00:31:02.870 --> 00:31:05.060
AUDIENCE: If you're the only
person who has money, then you

00:31:05.060 --> 00:31:05.360
can basically win.

00:31:05.360 --> 00:31:07.230
GUEST SPEAKER: You can basically
win if you're the

00:31:07.230 --> 00:31:08.090
only person who has
money, yeah.

00:31:08.090 --> 00:31:11.590
So it does require a little
bit of strategy, too.

00:31:11.590 --> 00:31:14.090
AUDIENCE: Technically,
from that set of--

00:31:14.090 --> 00:31:16.725
could you win on the first turn,
because it seems you

00:31:16.725 --> 00:31:20.640
only have two pieces
of a certain color.

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:22.630
AUDIENCE: Yeah, but
the right is still

00:31:22.630 --> 00:31:23.410
the starting position.

00:31:23.410 --> 00:31:24.250
AUDIENCE: Yeah, Iknow.

00:31:24.250 --> 00:31:29.140
And so on your turn, you could
take one-- your piece and have

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:31.640
it be moved to the square
right next to yours.

00:31:31.640 --> 00:31:32.200
AUDIENCE: No.

00:31:32.200 --> 00:31:33.520
AUDIENCE: Move to
any adjacent--

00:31:33.520 --> 00:31:35.290
AUDIENCE: Oh, you have
to move it--

00:31:35.290 --> 00:31:37.305
AUDIENCE: Read the rule
in the red there.

00:31:37.305 --> 00:31:39.125
AUDIENCE: Oh, any adjacent
number,okay.

00:31:39.125 --> 00:31:40.490
I didn't read the
adjacent part.

00:31:40.490 --> 00:31:42.203
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, it's any
adjacent or empty square.

00:31:42.203 --> 00:31:43.600
AUDIENCE: It's actually
really interesting.

00:31:43.600 --> 00:31:45.690
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, it's
a really weird--

00:31:45.690 --> 00:31:51.250
I also think you're not-- the
agency you have in the game to

00:31:51.250 --> 00:31:54.460
win is not by moving your own
pieces, it's by paying someone

00:31:54.460 --> 00:31:56.030
to do the work for you.

00:31:56.030 --> 00:31:58.448
So then we get back to this
quote at the beginning, "You

00:31:58.448 --> 00:31:59.344
load 16 tons and what
do you get?

00:31:59.344 --> 00:32:00.240
Another day older and
deeper in debt."

00:32:00.240 --> 00:32:02.720
It's like, what is
this game about.

00:32:02.720 --> 00:32:03.710
It could be about
a lot of things.

00:32:03.710 --> 00:32:06.670
And we honestly probably
shouldn't dissect it until

00:32:06.670 --> 00:32:08.610
we've played it.

00:32:08.610 --> 00:32:12.940
I think, too, the corrugated
cardboard, it gets hot inside

00:32:12.940 --> 00:32:14.330
of there, if you're
not outside.

00:32:14.330 --> 00:32:15.910
And that's a really interesting
thing, too.

00:32:15.910 --> 00:32:19.570
You're in there, it's hot, it's
sweaty, you've got dirty

00:32:19.570 --> 00:32:23.010
money in your hands, you're
moving around these sizable--

00:32:23.010 --> 00:32:24.249
they have weight to them.

00:32:24.249 --> 00:32:25.686
That was really important,
too.

00:32:25.686 --> 00:32:28.081
This could have been played
around a table with go-pieces

00:32:28.081 --> 00:32:29.518
or something, painted
go-pieces.

00:32:29.518 --> 00:32:34.077
But these are substantial steel
pieces that you've got

00:32:34.077 --> 00:32:36.040
to pick up and move.

00:32:36.040 --> 00:32:40.730
Obviously the song was
about manual labor.

00:32:40.730 --> 00:32:42.530
So this is a really
interesting game.

00:32:42.530 --> 00:32:44.270
This was released at the
Art History of Games

00:32:44.270 --> 00:32:45.420
conference last year.

00:32:45.420 --> 00:32:46.760
And it did well at IndieCade
this year.

00:32:46.760 --> 00:32:48.840
It won the Developers
Choice Award.

00:32:48.840 --> 00:32:50.890
And it's really cool.

00:32:50.890 --> 00:32:52.640
I want to try to get it
up to MIT so that we

00:32:52.640 --> 00:32:53.950
can all play it.

00:32:53.950 --> 00:32:57.030
AUDIENCE: It occurs to me that
if you went by rules where you

00:32:57.030 --> 00:32:59.400
keep the money you get to the
end of the game, you basically

00:32:59.400 --> 00:33:01.580
could throw the game to
get all the money.

00:33:01.580 --> 00:33:03.200
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah,
you could try.

00:33:03.200 --> 00:33:05.760
But people are gonna know
what you're doing.

00:33:05.760 --> 00:33:06.870
