00:33:36.250 --> 00:33:38.790
One thing I want to point out
with this-- we can talk after

00:33:38.790 --> 00:33:43.150
someone's played Gravitation,
a little bit about what we

00:33:43.150 --> 00:33:44.260
think about the game itself.

00:33:44.260 --> 00:33:45.660
But I think this quote
is interesting.

00:33:45.660 --> 00:33:47.980
He has artist statements
with all of his games.

00:33:47.980 --> 00:33:50.510
Some of them say more, some
of them say less.

00:33:50.510 --> 00:33:53.270
This one responded briefly
'cause this was after Passage

00:33:53.270 --> 00:33:53.590
was released.

00:33:53.590 --> 00:33:55.910
And I think you guys have
all seen Passage, right?

00:33:55.910 --> 00:33:58.740
You can immediately identify
the aesthetic similarities

00:33:58.740 --> 00:34:01.562
because the character
is exactly the same.

00:34:01.562 --> 00:34:04.900
He was responding to criticism
from people about his pixel

00:34:04.900 --> 00:34:07.435
art, and why did he
go for Pixel art.

00:34:07.435 --> 00:34:10.639
He says, "I now see ultra-low
res pixel art as a kind of

00:34:10.639 --> 00:34:12.159
digital cartooning.

00:34:12.159 --> 00:34:14.150
It stands right on the line
between the symbolic and the

00:34:14.150 --> 00:34:16.100
representational, leaving plenty
of room for viewer

00:34:16.100 --> 00:34:17.360
interpretation.

00:34:17.360 --> 00:34:20.565
Why draw sideburns when the
viewers can imagine sideburns

00:34:20.565 --> 00:34:21.539
on their own.

00:34:21.539 --> 00:34:22.876
You just need to give them
something to pin their

00:34:22.876 --> 00:34:24.948
imagination on, something
to guide them a bit.

00:34:24.948 --> 00:34:25.922
Cartoons are perfect for that.

00:34:25.922 --> 00:34:27.889
I think it's a really
interesting point.

00:34:27.889 --> 00:34:31.690
And I think what he's also
suggesting here, which is

00:34:31.690 --> 00:34:34.260
maybe not clear, is that this
is doing a similar thing to

00:34:34.260 --> 00:34:36.621
what that quote from
the music does.

00:34:36.621 --> 00:34:39.384
It's priming your experience
of the play.

00:34:39.384 --> 00:34:41.909
You load up this game, you see
that it's got this low-res

00:34:41.909 --> 00:34:44.320
pixel art that we now associate
with this kind of

00:34:44.320 --> 00:34:46.305
art game movement, and
you're ready to read

00:34:46.305 --> 00:34:47.889
this in another way.

00:34:47.889 --> 00:34:51.060
If I gave you a first-person
shooter and told you nothing

00:34:51.060 --> 00:34:52.900
about it except that it was
gonna be the fastest,

00:34:52.900 --> 00:34:54.469
awesomest, multiplayer
first-person shooter

00:34:54.469 --> 00:34:59.800
experience, but it looked like
this, would you be inclined to

00:34:59.800 --> 00:35:01.750
read it like an art game?

00:35:01.750 --> 00:35:02.900
I don't know.

00:35:02.900 --> 00:35:05.150
But certainly in this instance
with these kind of little

00:35:05.150 --> 00:35:08.500
games, this pixel-art aesthetic
that you see being

00:35:08.500 --> 00:35:14.110
duplicated in a lot of places,
is sort of priming the pump.

00:35:14.110 --> 00:35:17.340
It's establishing some sort
of context in which you

00:35:17.340 --> 00:35:18.580
can read this game.

