00:50:26.180 --> 00:50:28.420
Yeah, that's the idea.

00:50:28.420 --> 00:50:30.870
I think you guys have seen some
of the Molleindustria's

00:50:30.870 --> 00:50:32.138
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:50:32.138 --> 00:50:33.954
You guys saw Every Day
the Same Dream?

00:50:33.954 --> 00:50:34.792
AUDIENCE: I like that one.

00:50:34.792 --> 00:50:36.470
GUEST SPEAKER: Which
is interesting.

00:50:36.470 --> 00:50:39.940
Yeah, that one's also one
of my favorite games.

00:50:39.940 --> 00:50:47.350
This is looking at another kind
of sector of art, the

00:50:47.350 --> 00:50:49.135
activist political statement
sector of art is doing that

00:50:49.135 --> 00:50:52.040
through games, and it's
something-- this music too

00:50:52.040 --> 00:50:53.280
[INAUDIBLE]--

00:50:53.280 --> 00:50:56.240
something that he does
particularly

00:50:56.240 --> 00:50:56.990
well with his games.

00:50:56.990 --> 00:51:01.163
And I think this one's
particularly interesting and

00:51:01.163 --> 00:51:01.840
kind of funny.

00:51:01.840 --> 00:51:10.050
I love the reworking of a really
classic game mechanic.

00:51:10.050 --> 00:51:12.460
The entire point of memory is
your expectation that they

00:51:12.460 --> 00:51:15.590
would be the same the second
time you turn it over.

00:51:15.590 --> 00:51:19.420
And when it changes on you, it
really worsens that kind of

00:51:19.420 --> 00:51:24.660
perspective, I suppose
imbalance, that can exist when

00:51:24.660 --> 00:51:28.190
you're not just turning over
Toadstools and Bowsers, but

00:51:28.190 --> 00:51:30.700
actual, interesting things.

00:51:30.700 --> 00:51:36.180
I love the crying, sweating
polar bear with a beach chair.

00:51:36.180 --> 00:51:38.720
It actually cracks me up.

00:51:38.720 --> 00:51:39.750
Humor is actually
something that

00:51:39.750 --> 00:51:40.510
shouldn't be ignored here.

00:51:40.510 --> 00:51:43.260
If we remember, that was one of
the central Fluxus things,

00:51:43.260 --> 00:51:44.750
this idea of satire.

00:51:44.750 --> 00:51:47.230
Humor is important.

00:51:47.230 --> 00:51:51.972
So I think Paolo Pedercini's
work is really awesome.

00:51:51.972 --> 00:51:56.350
Again, for me, it's certainly
arcing up.

00:51:56.350 --> 00:51:57.820
This game's really cool.

00:51:57.820 --> 00:51:59.020
Who's played Love?

00:51:59.020 --> 00:52:00.345
A few people who's
played Love?

00:52:00.345 --> 00:52:01.015
All right, cool.

00:52:01.015 --> 00:52:04.168
I'd love someone who has not
played Love to play for a

00:52:04.168 --> 00:52:04.794
little bit.

00:52:04.794 --> 00:52:07.950
I don't know if we can
play the whole game.

00:52:07.950 --> 00:52:12.780
This is actually a game that I
really don't like, but I like

00:52:12.780 --> 00:52:15.170
the things that it does, if
that makes any sense.

00:52:15.170 --> 00:52:17.395
It bothers me for some reason.

00:52:17.395 --> 00:52:19.200
And yet--

00:52:19.200 --> 00:52:20.350
it's such an articulate
statement, this

00:52:20.350 --> 00:52:23.860
game bothers me--

00:52:23.860 --> 00:52:28.674
but I think it's worth looking
at, because he's doing a

00:52:28.674 --> 00:52:31.530
particularly interesting
thing, Alexander Ocias.

00:52:31.530 --> 00:52:34.865
I suppose it's a
soft C, Ocias.

00:52:43.773 --> 00:52:46.268
I have the sound turned off.

00:52:46.268 --> 00:52:47.765
I'm sorry, top right.

