AUDIENCE: Oh, I thought
that was bad.

00:56:39.320 --> 00:56:40.920
GUEST SPEAKER: This game is
well aggravatingly tuned,

00:56:40.920 --> 00:56:43.872
because it's difficult enough.

00:56:54.696 --> 00:56:57.156
AUDIENCE: Are you doing
that intentionally?

00:56:57.156 --> 00:56:58.406
AUDIENCE: I just like to.

00:57:03.590 --> 00:57:05.470
GUEST SPEAKER: It's actually
a pretty long game.

00:57:05.470 --> 00:57:07.920
I encourage you guys to--

00:57:07.920 --> 00:57:09.720
it's pretty- it's
all flash-based.

00:57:09.720 --> 00:57:11.549
AUDIENCE: What's the message?

00:57:11.549 --> 00:57:13.545
GUEST SPEAKER: It's a really
good question.

00:57:13.545 --> 00:57:15.042
Let me just jump in and go back
to that place where--

00:57:15.042 --> 00:57:17.038
thank you for playing.

00:57:17.038 --> 00:57:19.540
Thank you for playing.

00:57:19.540 --> 00:57:20.350
AUDIENCE: Disgusting.

00:57:20.350 --> 00:57:23.920
AUDIENCE: You are a changed
person because of it.

00:57:23.920 --> 00:57:26.150
So the question then becomes
what's the point, which

00:57:26.150 --> 00:57:28.060
already we're in a position
now where we're asking

00:57:28.060 --> 00:57:28.910
questions like that.

00:57:28.910 --> 00:57:31.330
Which is, if you were playing
Modern Warfare II, you might

00:57:31.330 --> 00:57:33.350
say what's the point, and
be like, to blow a lot

00:57:33.350 --> 00:57:35.210
of stuff up, right?

00:57:35.210 --> 00:57:38.850
This is the response he had in
one of his interviews he gave

00:57:38.850 --> 00:57:40.690
after his release and said, I
wanted to get people to wake

00:57:40.690 --> 00:57:42.830
up and think about the games
they are playing.

00:57:42.830 --> 00:57:44.680
Much of the industry appears to
be pushing in the opposite

00:57:44.680 --> 00:57:46.790
direction, as we'll have
tutorials, holding your hand

00:57:46.790 --> 00:57:49.650
from beginning to end in
subtext, screens for pointless

00:57:49.650 --> 00:57:50.540
exposition.

00:57:50.540 --> 00:57:52.320
It is treating players
like idiots.

00:57:52.320 --> 00:57:53.480
It is absolutely infuriating.

00:57:53.480 --> 00:57:55.960
When you think about it in terms
of the tutorial, if this

00:57:55.960 --> 00:57:57.066
is the tutorial for playing the

00:57:57.066 --> 00:57:59.370
game, it's pretty obnoxious.

00:57:59.370 --> 00:58:01.240
But it's not that much different
than the kind of

00:58:01.240 --> 00:58:03.600
tutorials we encounter in
a lot of games that just

00:58:03.600 --> 00:58:06.440
basically hold your hand and
tell you how to do everything.

00:58:06.440 --> 00:58:10.290
I think he's really
confronting that.

00:58:10.290 --> 00:58:12.140
I guess if I were to say the
reasons that I don't like this

00:58:12.140 --> 00:58:14.790
game, I think the control
scheme is

00:58:14.790 --> 00:58:17.040
actually, it's a weakness.

00:58:17.040 --> 00:58:21.242
The game is so hard that, I
mean, you saw you couldn't

00:58:21.242 --> 00:58:22.475
even get to the things that he
was trying to communicate.

00:58:22.475 --> 00:58:24.610
It gets weirder, the further
along you get,

00:58:24.610 --> 00:58:25.530
I can promise you.

00:58:25.530 --> 00:58:29.610
It's just you have to get really
skilled at doing it.

00:58:29.610 --> 00:58:34.480
There's kind of like a play
barrier for entry on it.

00:58:34.480 --> 00:58:40.856
It's also, for me, I think dare
I say, it almost gets too

00:58:40.856 --> 00:58:43.818
abstract for me at
certain points.

00:58:43.818 --> 00:58:46.220
Clearly he's trying to say
something, but there's nothing

00:58:46.220 --> 00:58:48.140
I can latch onto to understand
what it is.

00:58:48.140 --> 00:58:50.420
That's entirely his prerogative,
but for me, I'm

00:58:50.420 --> 00:58:52.290
just like, what?

00:58:52.290 --> 00:58:55.760
I have no clue what this
is about at all.

00:58:55.760 --> 00:58:59.045
So that's a little tricky,
not fully grasping.

00:58:59.045 --> 00:59:00.905
Clearly I'm not--

00:59:00.905 --> 00:59:02.950
I'm partially, so it's
like the perfect

00:59:02.950 --> 00:59:05.820
Fluxus piece of art.

00:59:05.820 --> 00:59:07.980
OK, so I've only got a couple
more to talk about.

00:59:07.980 --> 00:59:09.357
I've got about 15 minutes.

00:59:09.357 --> 00:59:11.422
This, I think, is actually one
of the most important games we

00:59:11.422 --> 00:59:13.490
can talk about today, which
is Cow Clicker.

00:59:13.490 --> 00:59:14.320
Who's played Cow Clicker?

00:59:14.320 --> 00:59:16.310
Yay.

00:59:16.310 --> 00:59:20.260
This is by Ian Bogost-- that's
the guy we saw earlier.

00:59:20.260 --> 00:59:22.240
And he said, Cow Clicker
is a Facebook game

00:59:22.240 --> 00:59:23.900
about Facebook games.

00:59:23.900 --> 00:59:26.470
It's partly a satire and partly
a playable theory of

00:59:26.470 --> 00:59:29.000
today's social games, and partly
an earnest example of

00:59:29.000 --> 00:59:30.350
that genre.

00:59:30.350 --> 00:59:32.370
So this is kind of like
a classic sort of

00:59:32.370 --> 00:59:33.520
art thing to do.

00:59:33.520 --> 00:59:36.310
You take something that you're
going to satire, and you make

00:59:36.310 --> 00:59:40.770
a version of that, that maybe
does something differently,

00:59:40.770 --> 00:59:43.190
but it's fundamentally what
you are satiring.

00:59:43.190 --> 00:59:46.620
It's like the Andy Kaufman
comedic kind of style.

00:59:46.620 --> 00:59:50.460
You just assume that kind
of character or role.

00:59:50.460 --> 00:59:57.150
It's something that I think is
like joyfully subversive in

00:59:57.150 --> 00:59:58.120
doing satire.

00:59:58.120 --> 01:00:00.110
So hopefully I'm logged into
Facebook so you can see.

01:00:00.110 --> 01:00:01.587
I haven't clicked recently,
so you guys can

01:00:01.587 --> 01:00:03.610
witness one of my clicks.

01:00:03.610 --> 01:00:04.860
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:00:07.047 --> 01:00:09.011
Facebook is down.

01:00:09.011 --> 01:00:10.975
Facebook is canceled forever.

01:00:10.975 --> 01:00:12.225
AUDIENCE: Yes.

01:00:14.920 --> 01:00:18.770
[LAUGHTER]

01:00:18.770 --> 01:00:20.400
It actually has no friends.

01:00:20.400 --> 01:00:22.720
It's also possible that
the wireless--

01:00:22.720 --> 01:00:25.560
the wireless in that corner
is kind of bad.

01:00:25.560 --> 01:00:26.810
GUEST SPEAKER: Should I switch
to Google Guest?

01:00:34.000 --> 01:00:36.610
I can actually, before we can
see it, I can talk a little

01:00:36.610 --> 01:00:38.740
bit about the sort of
interesting controversy that

01:00:38.740 --> 01:00:41.410
Cow Clicker is creating.

01:00:41.410 --> 01:00:43.040
It's making money.

01:00:48.050 --> 01:00:51.585
Let me try Google Guest.

01:00:51.585 --> 01:00:53.070
I'm not connected.

01:00:53.070 --> 01:00:55.050
How about now?

01:00:55.050 --> 01:00:58.590
It seems to be working better.

01:00:58.590 --> 01:00:59.840
AUDIENCE: You're still not--

01:01:03.030 --> 01:01:05.130
GUEST SPEAKER: The last part of
his statement is about an

01:01:05.130 --> 01:01:06.580
earnest effort at a game.

01:01:06.580 --> 01:01:08.340
It is fundamentally
a Facebook game.

01:01:08.340 --> 01:01:14.170
You can see I've got 5 mooney
and my next click is in a

01:01:14.170 --> 01:01:15.920
minute and 40-- ah, man, I
missed one of my clicks.

01:01:15.920 --> 01:01:18.240
But maybe if we wait while I
talk about it you can witness

01:01:18.240 --> 01:01:20.170
one of my clicks.

01:01:20.170 --> 01:01:22.490
Oh, wait, I actually have
some clicks left.

01:01:22.490 --> 01:01:24.120
I've got one this week.

01:01:24.120 --> 01:01:27.010
AUDIENCE: That's in two hours?

01:01:27.010 --> 01:01:28.030
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, it
looks like that's when

01:01:28.030 --> 01:01:29.630
I get my next one.

01:01:29.630 --> 01:01:31.475
So basically you can
click your cow.

01:01:35.570 --> 01:01:40.220
Why can I not click
my cow here.

01:01:40.220 --> 01:01:42.260
But you can click your cow, and
then you can-- oh, it's

01:01:42.260 --> 01:01:43.510
because I'm on scroll?

01:01:46.400 --> 01:01:48.380
Anyway, you can click your cow,
and then you can use your

01:01:48.380 --> 01:01:52.510
mooney to buy new cows that
are fundamentally the same

01:01:52.510 --> 01:01:56.945
thing as other cows, just with
aesthetic differences.

01:01:56.945 --> 01:01:59.960
So plain cow.

01:01:59.960 --> 01:02:00.930
These are the cows I have.

01:02:00.930 --> 01:02:02.350
I have a plain cow
and a steel cow.

01:02:02.350 --> 01:02:04.640
I haven't been playing
a lot of Cow Clicker.

01:02:04.640 --> 01:02:06.630
If I go to buy a cow,
I can buy a new cow.

01:02:09.270 --> 01:02:11.490
So you can see lots
of different cows.

01:02:11.490 --> 01:02:15.110
They cost you in
game currency.

01:02:15.110 --> 01:02:16.090
AUDIENCE: Paisley cow.

01:02:16.090 --> 01:02:19.190
GUEST SPEAKER: Holy cow.

01:02:19.190 --> 01:02:19.880
Oil cow [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

01:02:19.880 --> 01:02:22.180
It's got a BP logo.

01:02:22.180 --> 01:02:23.626
Cute.

01:02:23.626 --> 01:02:25.554
Like the [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
cows.

01:02:25.554 --> 01:02:27.000
That's pretty good.

01:02:27.000 --> 01:02:28.350
So there's lots of
different cows.

01:02:28.350 --> 01:02:30.820
You can buy cows, you can give
cows, you can invite a cow,

01:02:30.820 --> 01:02:32.680
there's cow rankings.

01:02:32.680 --> 01:02:34.450
AUDIENCE: You can buy a cow.

01:02:34.450 --> 01:02:37.780
GUEST SPEAKER: So you can see,
mooney's actually pretty

01:02:37.780 --> 01:02:38.910
inflated at this point.

01:02:38.910 --> 01:02:40.680
You can get 10 mooney for 50
cents, which is pretty good,

01:02:40.680 --> 01:02:44.060
1,000 for $10, which
is awesome.

01:02:44.060 --> 01:02:45.840
And collect a lot of cows.

01:02:45.840 --> 01:02:46.970
You can trade cows.

01:02:46.970 --> 01:02:49.460
And you can go to your friends
barns and see how

01:02:49.460 --> 01:02:51.200
many cows they have.

01:02:51.200 --> 01:02:55.070
And you can click on your cows
when you make your appointment

01:02:55.070 --> 01:02:56.850
to actually visit a cow.

01:02:56.850 --> 01:03:01.360
So I think you can see how he's
poking fun at things like

01:03:01.360 --> 01:03:03.735
FarmVille things like Ravenwood
Fair, which I'm

01:03:03.735 --> 01:03:07.750
obsessed with now, actually,
these kind of games where all

01:03:07.750 --> 01:03:10.565
you're fundamentally doing is
clicking and acquiring an

01:03:10.565 --> 01:03:12.020
in-game currency that's
you can then

01:03:12.020 --> 01:03:13.510
use for in-game goods.

01:03:13.510 --> 01:03:15.700
But then, of course, you reach
this barrier where you don't

01:03:15.700 --> 01:03:17.940
have enough in-game currency
and you use moo currency.

01:03:17.940 --> 01:03:20.820
Of course the interesting thing
is there are 500,000

01:03:20.820 --> 01:03:21.920
people playing this.

01:03:21.920 --> 01:03:22.430
I don't know if it's
that many.

01:03:22.430 --> 01:03:23.822
It's a lot.

01:03:23.822 --> 01:03:26.650
If you go to the Cow Clicker
front page it'll tell us.

01:03:26.650 --> 01:03:27.900
It just [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

01:03:30.361 --> 01:03:31.822
Where does it say how many
players there are?

01:03:31.822 --> 01:03:33.283
AUDIENCE: Ranking, probably
in ranking.

01:03:37.666 --> 01:03:41.080
He's milking the cow clicker.

01:03:41.080 --> 01:03:42.950
You can see this person's--

01:03:42.950 --> 01:03:44.830
Monica's--

01:03:44.830 --> 01:03:46.112
AUDIENCE: Is the cow--

01:03:48.620 --> 01:03:49.334
AUDIENCE: Is that their
all-time clicks

01:03:49.334 --> 01:03:50.910
or this week's clicks?

01:03:50.910 --> 01:03:52.770
AUDIENCE: Is there a--

01:03:52.770 --> 01:03:56.630
GUEST SPEAKER: Here
we go, yeah, yeah.

01:03:56.630 --> 01:03:57.840
12,000 monthly active users.

01:03:57.840 --> 01:04:00.410
That's 500,000 in a business
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

01:04:00.410 --> 01:04:01.490
But that's a lot of people.

01:04:01.490 --> 01:04:02.930
And he's making some interesting
money on it.

01:04:02.930 --> 01:04:04.680
And I think one of the things
that was important that he did

01:04:04.680 --> 01:04:06.290
is there's an active
forum where people

01:04:06.290 --> 01:04:07.495
can talk about it.

01:04:07.495 --> 01:04:10.500
So it is a little bit weirdly
controversial, 'cause he made

01:04:10.500 --> 01:04:13.320
this game that is poking
fun at Facebook games.

01:04:13.320 --> 01:04:16.935
And yet it is a Facebook game
and presumably making some

01:04:16.935 --> 01:04:18.960
money, too.

01:04:18.960 --> 01:04:21.115
But I think it's really good.

01:04:21.115 --> 01:04:23.640
This is the kind of art that's
also important, where it's

01:04:23.640 --> 01:04:27.930
taking what we do and purposely
looking at how is it

01:04:27.930 --> 01:04:32.080
constructed and how it's set up
and forcing us to kind of

01:04:32.080 --> 01:04:34.619
be introspective about the
things that we do and the way

01:04:34.619 --> 01:04:35.910
that we engage.

01:04:35.910 --> 01:04:38.340
So this is a kind of subversive
game design that I

01:04:38.340 --> 01:04:42.430
think along the lines of Paolo
Pedercini's work and even

01:04:42.430 --> 01:04:45.820
Gonzalo Frasca if forcing us
to look at how we interact.

01:04:45.820 --> 01:04:47.070
And Cow Clicker--

01:04:49.950 --> 01:04:50.620
So that's that one.

01:04:50.620 --> 01:04:51.880
And I've got like 15 minutes.

01:04:51.880 --> 01:04:55.340
Phillip asked me to talk a
little bit about some work

01:04:55.340 --> 01:04:58.450
that I'm doing in this space.

01:04:58.450 --> 01:05:01.210
So I'm gonna talk to you
about a game, it's

01:05:01.210 --> 01:05:02.670
still a work in progress.

01:05:02.670 --> 01:05:04.010
It's tentatively entitled
Before and

01:05:04.010 --> 01:05:07.950
Now, and it's by me.

01:05:07.950 --> 01:05:14.521
And the idea from it is it's
inspired by Fluxus boxes and

01:05:14.521 --> 01:05:16.710
those Flux kits.

01:05:16.710 --> 01:05:19.470
I'm really interested in
physically tangible games.

01:05:19.470 --> 01:05:22.370
I don't know why I'm all
of a sudden obsessed

01:05:22.370 --> 01:05:23.370
with holding something.

01:05:23.370 --> 01:05:26.790
But I was at IndieCade and I
heard again-- and this is the

01:05:26.790 --> 01:05:31.385
question that drives me more
nuts than the are video games

01:05:31.385 --> 01:05:33.940
art question, although they're
interestingly related.

01:05:33.940 --> 01:05:37.160
But it's when will we have the
Citizen Kane in video games?

01:05:37.160 --> 01:05:38.390
It drives me absolutely nuts.

01:05:38.390 --> 01:05:41.000
I think 80% of the time people
who say that have never seen

01:05:41.000 --> 01:05:42.830
Citizen Kane.

01:05:42.830 --> 01:05:44.720
I don't really know that.

01:05:44.720 --> 01:05:47.220
But I heard it for the umpteenth
time at IndieCade.

01:05:47.220 --> 01:05:50.015
I wanted to throw the television
out of my hotel

01:05:50.015 --> 01:05:51.120
room window.

01:05:51.120 --> 01:05:52.350
And I was going nuts.

01:05:52.350 --> 01:05:55.630
And I was like, all right, what
is Citizen Kane about?

01:05:55.630 --> 01:05:56.780
I don't want to spoil it.

01:05:56.780 --> 01:05:59.160
Who's seen Citizen Kane
in this room actually?

01:05:59.160 --> 01:06:01.680
All right, good.

01:06:01.680 --> 01:06:08.060
So I see Citizen Kane and it's
about this very interesting

01:06:08.060 --> 01:06:14.595
idea about presents and mementos
and happiness in a

01:06:14.595 --> 01:06:15.590
very kind of real way.

01:06:15.590 --> 01:06:17.710
And the temporality
of happiness, like

01:06:17.710 --> 01:06:18.690
are you happy now?

01:06:18.690 --> 01:06:19.630
Were you happy then?

01:06:19.630 --> 01:06:20.640
Do you remember being happy?

01:06:20.640 --> 01:06:22.820
When is happiness?

01:06:22.820 --> 01:06:25.150
And so I was like, well, I'm
going to make a Citizen Kane

01:06:25.150 --> 01:06:28.810
in video games, but it's not
gonna be known for being

01:06:28.810 --> 01:06:32.690
epically innovative with
camera movement.

01:06:32.690 --> 01:06:34.240
It's going to be about
the things I think

01:06:34.240 --> 01:06:36.760
Citizen Kane are about.

01:06:36.760 --> 01:06:42.590
So it's basically in this form,
although I'm rethinking

01:06:42.590 --> 01:06:44.290
what kind of box it's
gonna be in.

01:06:44.290 --> 01:06:45.760
But in this form it's a box.

01:06:45.760 --> 01:06:49.760
And inside of it are various
objects that start out as--

01:06:49.760 --> 01:06:51.900
the starting objects of the box
are the things that have

01:06:51.900 --> 01:06:53.900
personal significance to me.

01:06:53.900 --> 01:06:55.980
One of the rules of the game,
in fact, I think the most

01:06:55.980 --> 01:06:58.305
important rule of the
game is this one.

01:06:58.305 --> 01:07:00.710
Rule number one, you may
permanently exchange an object

01:07:00.710 --> 01:07:03.580
of personal significance for
any object of equal value.

01:07:03.580 --> 01:07:05.240
So when you're playing this
game, you can take something

01:07:05.240 --> 01:07:07.770
that belongs to you, and you can
exchange it for anything

01:07:07.770 --> 01:07:09.370
in the box.

01:07:09.370 --> 01:07:13.560
And of course, the interesting
thing is these objects in the

01:07:13.560 --> 01:07:17.350
box are of varying degrees
of interactivity.

01:07:17.350 --> 01:07:18.590
You can [INAUDIBLE]

01:07:18.590 --> 01:07:23.050
so you can draw something
on the Etch A Sketch.

01:07:23.050 --> 01:07:25.050
The rules are kind of veiled
at this moment now, where's

01:07:25.050 --> 01:07:26.890
there's like a couple
other rules that

01:07:26.890 --> 01:07:27.900
are causing to reflect.

01:07:27.900 --> 01:07:31.050
This is actually an extension
of Yup on Word which is a

01:07:31.050 --> 01:07:33.430
project we worked on in the
summer, which was a game that

01:07:33.430 --> 01:07:35.680
as you're playing it's a
platform where you're typing,

01:07:35.680 --> 01:07:38.440
and the game stops and asks
you personal questions.

01:07:38.440 --> 01:07:40.480
What I really liked about that
is it was doing something that

01:07:40.480 --> 01:07:45.982
I was referring to as a mechanic
of reflection, where

01:07:45.982 --> 01:07:50.440
the activity of playing is
reflecting, is thinking, is

01:07:50.440 --> 01:07:51.800
contemplating.

01:07:51.800 --> 01:07:56.120
It's not interactive in a very
active way as we think of it,

01:07:56.120 --> 01:07:57.470
it's more reflective.

01:07:57.470 --> 01:07:59.950
I liken it to the moments
in chess where you're

01:07:59.950 --> 01:08:00.640
planning your move.

01:08:00.640 --> 01:08:02.530
There's not a lot of
action in chess.

01:08:02.530 --> 01:08:04.740
But it's a lot of contemplation
in chess.

01:08:04.740 --> 01:08:06.290
So if it's personal--

01:08:06.290 --> 01:08:07.820
what if that were personal.

01:08:07.820 --> 01:08:11.590
And so what I didn't
particularly care about with

01:08:11.590 --> 01:08:16.420
Yup on Word it inspires you to
reflect, but it doesn't do it

01:08:16.420 --> 01:08:18.380
with tremendous grace.

01:08:18.380 --> 01:08:19.540
It just asks you a question.

01:08:19.540 --> 01:08:23.210
It just comes up out of the blue
and and says, hey, when

01:08:23.210 --> 01:08:24.830
was the last time you lied?

01:08:24.830 --> 01:08:27.250
Sometimes you're surprised by
that, and that's a good

01:08:27.250 --> 01:08:30.319
effect, but it's also
not subtle.

01:08:30.319 --> 01:08:33.279
So I'm trying to explore how
can we be subtle in causing

01:08:33.279 --> 01:08:34.109
reflection.

01:08:34.109 --> 01:08:35.180
So there are a few
things here.

01:08:35.180 --> 01:08:36.750
The value question
is interesting.

01:08:36.750 --> 01:08:39.890
How do you know what the value
of the object is in the box?

01:08:39.890 --> 01:08:42.210
Do you think of it as
monetary value?

01:08:42.210 --> 01:08:44.300
Do you think of it as value in
terms of significance to the

01:08:44.300 --> 01:08:45.950
person who put that in there?

01:08:45.950 --> 01:08:48.649
Why is it significant to the
person who put that in there?

01:08:48.649 --> 01:08:49.790
What is this thing?

01:08:49.790 --> 01:08:50.729
You can look at it
and reflect.

01:08:50.729 --> 01:08:54.090
And then when you leave a trace
of yourself in this box,

01:08:54.090 --> 01:08:57.100
the next person who comes and
plays is going to see that.

01:08:57.100 --> 01:08:58.979
And they're going to wonder
what was that.

01:08:58.979 --> 01:09:06.810
So I'm also kind of interested
the organicness, the kind of

01:09:06.810 --> 01:09:10.180
never-ending, interminable
nature of a game that carries

01:09:10.180 --> 01:09:14.240
the effect of the person who's
played it on and on and on.

01:09:14.240 --> 01:09:16.519
The next iteration of this that
I want to do-- actually

01:09:16.519 --> 01:09:18.819
this was an idea that Jason
Bean gave me was to use an

01:09:18.819 --> 01:09:20.200
apothecary chest.

01:09:20.200 --> 01:09:22.279
Because one of the things that
we play-tested it here with--

01:09:22.279 --> 01:09:24.523
I left it out for two weeks
and people-- you

01:09:24.523 --> 01:09:25.430
probably saw it.

01:09:25.430 --> 01:09:25.939
It's right there.

01:09:25.939 --> 01:09:27.074
People were engaging in--

01:09:27.074 --> 01:09:31.020
this is a photo with objects
that people had left in.

01:09:31.020 --> 01:09:33.326
So it was really fun for me
after two weeks to go into

01:09:33.326 --> 01:09:36.300
this box and be like, what
is all this stuff.

01:09:36.300 --> 01:09:37.210
What got left?

01:09:37.210 --> 01:09:39.109
I was actually surprised the
Etch A Sketch was left.

01:09:39.109 --> 01:09:40.920
And then I was like,
what is this stuff?

01:09:40.920 --> 01:09:43.963
And to give you one really good
example, one person at

01:09:43.963 --> 01:09:49.970
the lab put in a Nintendo DS
flash card, which was really a

01:09:49.970 --> 01:09:51.660
wrapped DS flash card.

01:09:51.660 --> 01:09:54.130
So now there's this kind
of next level.

01:09:54.130 --> 01:09:55.810
The game has changed with
another level of

01:09:55.810 --> 01:09:59.340
interactivity, where it's like,
if I want to get at what

01:09:59.340 --> 01:10:03.630
was the significance of this
object to this person, I need

01:10:03.630 --> 01:10:06.110
to get a DS and play this.

01:10:06.110 --> 01:10:10.920
So I open up the-- it's got
folders, and it's got a copy

01:10:10.920 --> 01:10:12.130
of the game Lemming.

01:10:12.130 --> 01:10:13.870
It's got some music in it.

01:10:13.870 --> 01:10:15.610
So now it's kind of like you've

01:10:15.610 --> 01:10:16.940
uncovered this next layer.

01:10:16.940 --> 01:10:19.980
And it's like the interesting
traces of the people who

01:10:19.980 --> 01:10:24.990
played this game are now marked
onto this box forever.

01:10:24.990 --> 01:10:26.140
Marley even said to me--

01:10:26.140 --> 01:10:29.020
I was talking to her about her
play after she had done it,

01:10:29.020 --> 01:10:30.940
and I was like--

01:10:30.940 --> 01:10:33.660
She told me she wanted to
take the instructions.

01:10:33.660 --> 01:10:34.680
She wanted to take the rules.

01:10:34.680 --> 01:10:36.800
That's one of the objects that
she could take out of that.

01:10:36.800 --> 01:10:38.610
And I told her I had thought
about that actually when I was

01:10:38.610 --> 01:10:40.474
designing it, that someone might
be like, ha, I'm going

01:10:40.474 --> 01:10:41.160
to take the rules.

01:10:41.160 --> 01:10:43.660
And I was going to put in
a rule that said you can

01:10:43.660 --> 01:10:46.120
permanently exchange for an
object that's not the rules,

01:10:46.120 --> 01:10:47.640
so that the game
could persist.

01:10:47.640 --> 01:10:51.010
But I didn't want to
artificially impose that.

01:10:51.010 --> 01:10:53.050
If someone takes the rules
because that's what they want,

01:10:53.050 --> 01:10:54.780
and now the game has changed.

01:10:54.780 --> 01:10:58.060
And maybe someone comes along
later and modifies it.

01:10:58.060 --> 01:11:00.000
And maybe they put something in
that makes it a whole new,

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:00.650
different game.

01:11:00.650 --> 01:11:03.410
And I really liked the idea that
you could encounter this

01:11:03.410 --> 01:11:07.110
game and that it's gonna be in
this state of flux, I guess

01:11:07.110 --> 01:11:09.430
you could say.

01:11:09.430 --> 01:11:10.810
That's what I'm working
on now.

01:11:10.810 --> 01:11:13.095
And like I said, I think one of
the things I didn't like is

01:11:13.095 --> 01:11:15.600
that all of the objects are
accessible all at once.

01:11:15.600 --> 01:11:18.150
So if it's an apothecary chest
with lots of drawers, this was

01:11:18.150 --> 01:11:20.590
Jason Bean's idea, then you have
to do a little exploring.

01:11:20.590 --> 01:11:22.860
And maybe the rules are in
one of those drawers.

01:11:22.860 --> 01:11:24.280
And maybe you see
the rules first.

01:11:24.280 --> 01:11:25.730
Maybe you see the rules
way after you've

01:11:25.730 --> 01:11:26.350
looked at a lot of stuff.

01:11:26.350 --> 01:11:28.280
And now you've seen all this
stuff and played with it all

01:11:28.280 --> 01:11:29.435
and you really like something.

01:11:29.435 --> 01:11:32.520
And now it's like, ooh,
what do I trade?

01:11:32.520 --> 01:11:38.560
And yeah, I also am trying to
find a way to give the box a

01:11:38.560 --> 01:11:39.960
history as well.

01:11:39.960 --> 01:11:43.670
I've contemplated some ideas
of putting in some kind of

01:11:43.670 --> 01:11:47.230
clues that you could then, if
you felt, if you thought about

01:11:47.230 --> 01:11:49.040
it or looked it up, you might
be able to find a

01:11:49.040 --> 01:11:50.745
story to this box.

01:11:50.745 --> 01:11:52.710
Like if you did an Internet
search you might come up on a

01:11:52.710 --> 01:11:55.000
fake website-- well, it wouldn't
be fake, it'd just be

01:11:55.000 --> 01:11:58.800
a website-- but a website with
a fake story that tells you a

01:11:58.800 --> 01:12:00.893
little bit about this box, and
then it gives you a few more

01:12:00.893 --> 01:12:03.200
hooks into what the meaning of
this game or the inspiration

01:12:03.200 --> 01:12:05.740
for this game was.

01:12:05.740 --> 01:12:07.850
And so this is one of my
projects that I'm working on

01:12:07.850 --> 01:12:10.430
right now, like I said, I'm
interested in essentially.

01:12:10.430 --> 01:12:13.460
But you can see where a lot of
the other things that I was

01:12:13.460 --> 01:12:17.700
talking about are inspiring
this work.

01:12:17.700 --> 01:12:22.415
And so we come back to that
question of, are games art?

01:12:22.415 --> 01:12:23.410
Of course we only have
five minutes.

01:12:23.410 --> 01:12:24.380
I don't know if you guys have
administrative stuff

01:12:24.380 --> 01:12:24.840
afterwards.

01:12:24.840 --> 01:12:31.120
But this is, like I said,
this is my viewpoint.

01:12:31.120 --> 01:12:34.690
It's certainly shared with
a lot of people.

01:12:34.690 --> 01:12:39.093
But it's not guaranteed truth.

01:12:39.093 --> 01:12:44.470
This is an opinion that
I have just shared.

01:12:44.470 --> 01:12:48.500
Any thoughts you guys have about
this subject or these

01:12:48.500 --> 01:12:51.222
ideas or these games?

01:12:51.222 --> 01:12:54.680
Anything at all?

01:12:54.680 --> 01:12:57.200
Yesterday's football game?

01:12:57.200 --> 01:12:58.450
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:13:01.460 --> 01:13:02.110
GUEST SPEAKER: Well, cool.

01:13:02.110 --> 01:13:03.230
I hope this was useful.

01:13:03.230 --> 01:13:05.000
I hope it was helpful.

01:13:05.000 --> 01:13:08.280
I think if, at the very least,
it causes you to continue to

01:13:08.280 --> 01:13:10.920
think about your own games that
you're developing and how

01:13:10.920 --> 01:13:14.660
meaning can be embedded in the
play that you might not even

01:13:14.660 --> 01:13:16.080
realize is there.

01:13:16.080 --> 01:13:17.830
And also just to count the
possibility space.

01:13:17.830 --> 01:13:19.510
I really am excited by--

01:13:19.510 --> 01:13:23.710
I'm discouraged by the safe
choices that a lot of

01:13:23.710 --> 01:13:25.675
independent game developers
make and excited when some

01:13:25.675 --> 01:13:29.160
game developers really
push on the edge.

01:13:29.160 --> 01:13:30.960
I don't think we have a clear
understanding of what the

01:13:30.960 --> 01:13:34.190
rules are or what even
constitutes a game.

01:13:34.190 --> 01:13:37.010
Or what degree of interactivity
is appropriate

01:13:37.010 --> 01:13:38.800
for a quote/unquote game.

01:13:38.800 --> 01:13:42.260
And I think some of these
designers are really testing

01:13:42.260 --> 01:13:45.072
the waters of the medium,
you know, what can we

01:13:45.072 --> 01:13:45.990
really do with this?

01:13:45.990 --> 01:13:47.760
Where can we go?

01:13:47.760 --> 01:13:49.975
How edgy and satirical and
avant-garde can we be?

01:13:53.090 --> 01:13:55.571
I think that's always good.

01:13:55.571 --> 01:13:59.954
'Cause I'm a revolutionary,
crazy person.

01:13:59.954 --> 01:14:01.204
AUDIENCE: Crazy person.

01:14:07.760 --> 01:14:11.280
AUDIENCE: Hope 7 played yet one
more this year, which were

01:14:11.280 --> 01:14:14.568
some of the games that Abe
worked with the team on over

01:14:14.568 --> 01:14:15.570
the summer.

01:14:15.570 --> 01:14:19.492
Those are all on our GAMBIT
website, just until 2010 and

01:14:19.492 --> 01:14:21.690
as yet [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
which we're

01:14:21.690 --> 01:14:23.220
booked about this summer.

01:14:23.220 --> 01:14:26.480
This sounds like tri-season
will be digital work.

01:14:26.480 --> 01:14:27.790
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, and please
play them and give us

01:14:27.790 --> 01:14:28.620
feedback, too, right?

01:14:28.620 --> 01:14:29.784
Because that's the
other thing.

01:14:29.784 --> 01:14:32.860
These are not perfect
articulations of game as the

01:14:32.860 --> 01:14:34.654
pure art form.

01:14:34.654 --> 01:14:37.039
We're constantly evolving and
trying to improve, so any

01:14:37.039 --> 01:14:39.435
feedback you can give us on
this stuff is greatly

01:14:39.435 --> 01:14:40.685
appreciated.

01:14:42.420 --> 01:14:44.520
AUDIENCE: Do you still have
that box, like somewhere?

01:14:44.520 --> 01:14:45.100
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, yeah.

01:14:45.100 --> 01:14:45.805
It's in my office.

01:14:45.805 --> 01:14:46.250
Yeah, totally.

01:14:46.250 --> 01:14:50.970
I have the box and the other
part of it is when I do the

01:14:50.970 --> 01:14:54.760
second version of it in the
apothecary kind of chest--

01:14:54.760 --> 01:14:57.620
The one thing that's important
to me is that each of the--

01:14:57.620 --> 01:15:00.470
For the prototype I just sort
of threw some reasonably

01:15:00.470 --> 01:15:02.210
meaningful objects in there.

01:15:02.210 --> 01:15:05.020
But I want it to be populated
with objects that are

01:15:05.020 --> 01:15:08.350
personally significant to myself
or symbolic to myself.

01:15:08.350 --> 01:15:13.270
And populating that box at the
outset with meaningful objects

01:15:13.270 --> 01:15:19.050
I think is important, because
once it's released into the

01:15:19.050 --> 01:15:22.100
world for play that it's
constantly changing.

01:15:22.100 --> 01:15:27.460
So that first instantiation of
the game is the one that's

01:15:27.460 --> 01:15:30.210
about me in some sense, and
about my experiences and then

01:15:30.210 --> 01:15:32.520
it becomes the world's
to just play.

01:15:32.520 --> 01:15:36.210
AUDIENCE: So the way that your
game kind of reminds me of is

01:15:36.210 --> 01:15:38.740
I'm pretty sure I heard
somewhere that some

01:15:38.740 --> 01:15:41.950
person had a pen.

01:15:41.950 --> 01:15:44.824
He started with a pen
and kept trading--

01:15:44.824 --> 01:15:46.290
AUDIENCE: The red paper--

01:15:46.290 --> 01:15:46.980
AUDIENCE: No.

01:15:46.980 --> 01:15:48.210
This is a different one.

01:15:48.210 --> 01:15:49.500
Not the red paper
clip for a car.

01:15:49.500 --> 01:15:52.120
I think someone started with
a pen or something and kept

01:15:52.120 --> 01:15:52.450
trading it.

01:15:52.450 --> 01:15:57.140
And over the course of a
year or two, he'd got--

01:15:57.140 --> 01:16:00.314
From that initial pen,
he was able to trade

01:16:00.314 --> 01:16:01.640
for a house or something.

01:16:01.640 --> 01:16:03.940
GUEST SPEAKER: It was
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

01:16:03.940 --> 01:16:06.980
And that was sort of
an interesting--

01:16:06.980 --> 01:16:09.860
I think it was on eBay, too,
or something like that.

01:16:09.860 --> 01:16:12.460
And that's even using a pretty
well-designed game system--

01:16:12.460 --> 01:16:14.560
'cause eBay is totally
a game--

01:16:14.560 --> 01:16:17.850
and using a game system and
subverting the system itself

01:16:17.850 --> 01:16:19.970
to do something really
interesting.

01:16:19.970 --> 01:16:21.170
Yeah, that's a really great--

01:16:21.170 --> 01:16:23.190
it's a game inside of a game.

01:16:23.190 --> 01:16:23.820
I love that.

01:16:23.820 --> 01:16:26.320
It's kind of like the Cow
Clicker, like making a game

01:16:26.320 --> 01:16:29.612
inside of the game
that subverts--

01:16:29.612 --> 01:16:31.430
AUDIENCE: Are you familiar
with the concept of

01:16:31.430 --> 01:16:33.500
geo-caching?

01:16:33.500 --> 01:16:35.596
When I first saw--

01:16:35.596 --> 01:16:37.488
the game you were talking about
using [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:16:37.488 --> 01:16:38.907
reminded me of it.

01:16:38.907 --> 01:16:40.700
So initially what it was, was
when GPS's first came out,

01:16:40.700 --> 01:16:42.310
people were like, oh, hey,
this is really cool

01:16:42.310 --> 01:16:42.460
technology.

01:16:42.460 --> 01:16:44.190
We should think of something
fun to do with it.

01:16:44.190 --> 01:16:49.255
So what they did was, these
people started hiding empty

01:16:49.255 --> 01:16:53.770
tins of little trinkets and
stuff on various hiking trails

01:16:53.770 --> 01:16:55.080
or other locations.

01:16:55.080 --> 01:16:56.840
And you would look up the
coordinates on your GPS

01:16:56.840 --> 01:16:59.622
finder, and you would
find this box.

01:16:59.622 --> 01:17:02.646
And then you'd trade trinkets,
and you get them.

01:17:02.646 --> 01:17:05.020
And you keep traveling
to these other--

01:17:05.020 --> 01:17:06.330
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, and in
that sense this whole

01:17:06.330 --> 01:17:09.930
worldwide game becomes--

01:17:09.930 --> 01:17:12.690
There's some shared ideas with
some location-based games that

01:17:12.690 --> 01:17:17.110
do some really interesting
things with that, whether it's

01:17:17.110 --> 01:17:19.885
alternate reality, but not
even with the narrative.

01:17:19.885 --> 01:17:23.867
There was one I was reading
about that was, come out and

01:17:23.867 --> 01:17:26.850
play, I think, in New York
City at one point.

01:17:26.850 --> 01:17:29.280
It was Cruel to be Kind,
it was called.

01:17:29.280 --> 01:17:33.526
It was a game where you don't
know who's playing, you don't

01:17:33.526 --> 01:17:36.900
know who's on what team, but you
get missions and you get a

01:17:36.900 --> 01:17:40.630
weapon that's a complement, like
to kill someone who's on

01:17:40.630 --> 01:17:42.660
the other team, mistake them
for a celebrity, or

01:17:42.660 --> 01:17:44.110
something like that.

01:17:44.110 --> 01:17:46.740
So you're using these random
acts of kindness-- you know,

01:17:46.740 --> 01:17:48.140
helping somebody across
the street--

01:17:48.140 --> 01:17:51.672
and you don't know, it's in New
York City, so you don't

01:17:51.672 --> 01:17:52.580
know if someone's playing
the game.

01:17:52.580 --> 01:17:54.890
So now all of a sudden you're
wandering around doing really

01:17:54.890 --> 01:17:58.140
nice things for people on the
chance, on a hunch that you

01:17:58.140 --> 01:18:00.310
have that maybe they're
playing the game.

01:18:00.310 --> 01:18:01.830
And maybe they're not, so you've
just helped somebody

01:18:01.830 --> 01:18:03.777
across the street, which
is totally nice.

01:18:03.777 --> 01:18:07.156
And it comes to this massive
conclusion where you just have

01:18:07.156 --> 01:18:10.300
these two giant teams who meet
up in a location to "nice"

01:18:10.300 --> 01:18:13.520
each other to death,
basically.

01:18:13.520 --> 01:18:16.920
There's a lot of really, really
interesting stuff,

01:18:16.920 --> 01:18:20.370
especially in the
location-based, around.

01:18:20.370 --> 01:18:21.780
Big games are really cool.

01:18:21.780 --> 01:18:23.230
There's a lot of people
doing really neat

01:18:23.230 --> 01:18:25.550
stuff with big games.

01:18:25.550 --> 01:18:28.000
AUDIENCE: I think in particular
with the box and

01:18:28.000 --> 01:18:31.877
the geo-caching idea, and
talking about the

01:18:31.877 --> 01:18:35.740
paperclip-for-a-house thing,
it's really interesting how

01:18:35.740 --> 01:18:37.710
people value things.

01:18:37.710 --> 01:18:40.380
In particular, you have this
very vague rule, and it just

01:18:40.380 --> 01:18:42.690
says something of equal value.

01:18:42.690 --> 01:18:43.940
And what does it
actually mean?

01:18:46.910 --> 01:18:50.230
Like what someone is willing to
trade for something else.

01:18:50.230 --> 01:18:51.720
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, those are
the kind of implicit rules

01:18:51.720 --> 01:18:53.715
like you see in Sixteen Tons,
where it's like, what happens

01:18:53.715 --> 01:18:56.210
with the money?

01:18:56.210 --> 01:18:57.800
Jason Bean really pointed it
out, 'cause he was thinking

01:18:57.800 --> 01:18:59.400
about value when he was
doing the trading.

01:18:59.400 --> 01:19:01.530
And it's such a good word,
because value is such a

01:19:01.530 --> 01:19:03.110
different thing to
different people.

01:19:03.110 --> 01:19:08.190
Someone might see the flash card
for the DS, which has a

01:19:08.190 --> 01:19:12.330
very clear, higher monetary
value than an Etch A Sketch.

01:19:12.330 --> 01:19:14.500
And yet, maybe you played with
an Etch A Sketch when you were

01:19:14.500 --> 01:19:17.080
a kid, and all of a sudden it
floods back these memories

01:19:17.080 --> 01:19:19.110
that you have.

01:19:19.110 --> 01:19:21.670
There's also a component to it
that I didn't really talk

01:19:21.670 --> 01:19:23.815
about, which is the sacrificing
of an object.

01:19:23.815 --> 01:19:30.080
And I think, too, that we tend
to be beholden to our material

01:19:30.080 --> 01:19:32.400
objects too much.

01:19:32.400 --> 01:19:36.225
In giving it up, in sacrificing
it to this game so

01:19:36.225 --> 01:19:37.580
that you don't have it anymore,
if it's something of

01:19:37.580 --> 01:19:40.650
personal significance, you're
letting go and being able to

01:19:40.650 --> 01:19:42.040
say, OK, the memory
is one thing.

01:19:42.040 --> 01:19:43.170
I don't need to--

01:19:43.170 --> 01:19:46.355
And that's how it relates a
bit back to Citizen Kane,

01:19:46.355 --> 01:19:49.265
which is a movie you should
see, because it is good.

01:19:49.265 --> 01:19:54.115
It's everything they said it is,
depending on who they are.

01:19:58.490 --> 01:19:58.960
Cool.

01:19:58.960 --> 01:20:00.210
Thanks, guys.